   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Spatial Scale does not cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. There is no direct causal mechanism by which altering spatial scale would affect an individual's basal metabolic rate, nor does basal metabolic rate cause spatial scale to change. These variables operate independently within the system.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleiber's_law", 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4381225/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2602708/', 'https://www.khanacademy.org/science/ap-biology/ecology-ap/energy-flow-through-ecosystems/a/metabolic-rate', 'https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.012579799'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kleiber\'s_law", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Kleiber\'s law article describes how basal metabolic rate (BMR) scales with body mass (and mentions effects of temperature and taxonomic order) and discusses distinctions between resting (basal) and active metabolic rates. It does not provide evidence that larger spatial scale, territory, or range causally increases BMR; movement-related energy expenditure is treated as activity (not basal) and the page does not link territory size to higher baseline metabolism. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4381225/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper addresses how basal metabolic rate scales with body mass and the roles of cellular vs. systemic regulation (e.g., tissue composition, thermoregulation), not how \\"spatial scale\\" (territory or range size) causally increases BMR. It emphasizes negative allometry of mass-specific metabolic rate with body size and discusses biological regulation and resource‑supply limits, but does not provide evidence that organisms occupying larger territories have higher basal metabolic rates due to greater movement or resource acquisition demands. Thus the citation does not support the claimed positive causal relationship between spatial scale and BMR."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2602708/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited paper examines how metabolic rate scaling with body mass varies with metabolic/activity level (e.g., resting vs. field vs. maximal activity) and shows that activity level and metabolic state affect metabolic scaling and field/metabolic rates. It does not provide evidence that organisms occupying larger territories or spatial scales causally have higher basal metabolic rates (BMR) because of larger ranges; BMR is measured at rest and the paper separates resting/BMR from activity- or field-related metabolic rates. The citation therefore does not support the specific causal claim that larger spatial scale increases basal metabolic rate via greater movement/resource acquisition demands."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Larger spatial scales cause an increase in basal metabolic rate because organisms occupying larger territories or ranges need to expend more energy for movement and resource acquisition. This increased activity directly elevates their basal metabolic rate to support the demands of navigating and surviving in expansive environments.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Larger spatial scales cause an increase in basal metabolic rate because organisms occupying larger territories or ranges need to expend more energy for movement and resource acquisition. This increased activity directly elevates their basal metabolic rate to support the demands of navigating and surviving in expansive environments.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

