11. Individual Ideal BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Ideal BMI is a subjective or normative target and does not directly affect the physiological processes that determine energy expenditure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking an individual's ideal BMI target to their actual daily energy expenditure.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how actual body size and composition (fat‑free mass and fat mass), physical activity, age, sex, and genetics determine resting and total energy expenditure. It reports that REE and absolute TEE tend to increase with greater body mass and that differences in TEE between people with and without obesity largely disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass or body weight. It also discusses adaptive reductions in REE with weight loss, but it does not discuss an effect of an individual’s \\"ideal BMI\\" (a subjective preference) on TDEE, nor does it support a general inverse relationship where higher ideal BMI causes lower TDEE regardless of body composition or activity. Thus the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes how total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) is driven by resting metabolic rate (which scales with body mass and especially fat‑free mass), the thermic effect of food, and physical activity, and notes that becoming heavier tends to increase RMR and the energy cost of movement (i.e., TDEE goes up with greater body mass). It also discusses adaptive reductions in expenditure with weight loss, not increased efficiency at higher weights. The paper does not provide evidence that a higher \\"ideal BMI\\" causes lower TDEE or that people who prefer a higher BMI inherently burn fewer calories independent of actual body composition or activity. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited chapter describes that resting energy expenditure and total energy expenditure generally increase with body size/BMI (absolute REE and TEE are higher in individuals with greater body mass), and it emphasizes that body composition and physical activity are major determinants of energy expenditure. It also notes that differences often disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass. The sources do not provide evidence that a higher \\"ideal BMI\\" causes lower TDEE or that people with a higher ideal BMI \\"naturally burn fewer calories\\" regardless of actual body composition or activity. No discussion supports the unqualified notion of energy‑conserving efficiency tied to a subjective \\"ideal BMI.\\" \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy at higher ideal weights. This means that people with a higher ideal BMI naturally burn fewer calories throughout the day, regardless of their actual body composition or activity level.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy at higher ideal weights. This means that people with a higher ideal BMI naturally burn fewer calories throughout the day, regardless of their actual body composition or activity level.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

