   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, and its limitations and health implications, but it does not discuss individuals\' ideal BMI, body-image preferences, or evidence that a person\'s stated ideal BMI causally influences their eating/exercise behaviors or actual BMI. There is no support in the source for the claimed causal relationship or behavioral mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop proceedings discuss BMI as a measurement tool, its strengths and limitations, disease risk associations, and clinical communication/stigma around obesity. They do not present evidence that a person\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise behavior or that having a higher individual ideal BMI leads to a higher actual BMI. No data or analysis in the citation links personal ideal weight norms to subsequent changes in BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes what BMI is, its categories, clinical significance, and physiological/health associations of higher BMI, but does not present evidence that a person\'s stated or subconscious \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise to raise actual BMI. It contains no discussion or data on personal ideals, body-image goals, or behavioral mechanisms linking an individual\'s ideal BMI to changes in their real BMI. Therefore the explanation is unsupported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its clinical uses and limits, and how BMI thresholds guide screening, counseling, and eligibility for treatments. It does not provide evidence that an individual’s personally held “ideal BMI” causes them to subconsciously eat or exercise in ways that raise their actual BMI. While the paper notes people may use BMI for setting weight goals and describes social/psychological aspects of weight (e.g., stigma), it contains no empirical support for the specific causal claim that higher individual ideal BMI increases a person’s real BMI via behavioral adjustment."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article debates the utility and limits of BMI as a measurement, discusses historical/contextual issues, weight stigma, and population-level uses of BMI, but it does not provide empirical evidence that an individual’s personal \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their eating/exercise behavior or causes their actual BMI to increase. It raises that BMI and societal ideals can affect perceptions and stigma, but it does not support the specific causal claim that higher individual ideal BMI leads to higher real BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI directly causes a person’s actual Body Mass Index (BMI) to increase, as people tend to subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to match their ideal. Therefore, the higher one’s ideal BMI, the higher their real BMI will be.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI directly causes a person’s actual Body Mass Index (BMI) to increase, as people tend to subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to match their ideal. Therefore, the higher one’s ideal BMI, the higher their real BMI will be.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

