   Properties: {'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.26589447799265764, 'citations': ['https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-alone', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7139933690076048, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited URMC piece criticizes BMI as a blunt, historically contingent measure and emphasizes that weight-related outcomes are driven by complex behaviors, genetics, fitness, and social factors (including stigma). That supports the idea that effects linking individual beliefs about \\"ideal weight\\" to population BMI would likely be indirect and mediated by behavior/social influences. However, the article does not directly address whether an individual\'s ideal BMI can (or cannot) directly cause changes in group-level BMI, nor does it provide empirical evidence about causal pathways between individual ideals and group averages."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, and its limitations as an individual screening tool. It does not discuss individual \\"ideal BMI\\", group-level BMI, or causal pathways between an individual\'s ideal and changes in a group\'s average BMI. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the claimed absence of a direct causal effect.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.yalemedicine.org/news/why-you-shouldnt-rely-on-bmi-a