22. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study examines BMI in relation to physical fitness measures (explosive power, flexibility, cardiorespiratory endurance) and generally reports negative or null associations between BMI and fitness (higher BMI associated with poorer fitness). It does not provide evidence that greater physical activity leads to higher BMI via increased muscle mass; it mostly treats BMI as correlated with lower fitness and does not support a general positive causal link from activity to higher BMI. (The paper only notes isolated findings or hypotheses about muscle compensating for fat in specific strength tests, which do not justify the broad claim.)  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly notes that muscle and bone are denser than fat and that people undertaking exercise/fitness programs may gain muscle while losing fat, producing a stable or even increased BMI — i.e., BMI can rise with increased muscle mass and thus can misclassify muscular, active individuals as “overweight.” However, the paper frames this as a limitation of BMI and an explanation for misclassification in some individuals, not as evidence of a general population-level positive effect of greater physical activity causing higher BMI. Therefore the mechanism is supported but the broad causal claim (more activity → higher BMI overall) is not fully established by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited J Clin Med (2023) study reports that, in a sample of young medical students, moderate and vigorous physical activity correlated with greater fat‑free mass and bone mass and — notably in men — moderate activity was associated with higher body mass and BMI (the authors state the BMI increase reflected lean mass rather than fat). However, the evidence is cross‑sectional, from a small, specific sample, shows sex differences, and the paper itself notes contrasting findings in other (e.g., older/obese) populations. Thus the citation supports the claim only partially and only in a limited context rather than as a general causal rule.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls review discusses that resistance (anaerobic) exercise can increase muscle mass and notes that resistance training is encouraged to build muscle. However, the article\'s overall emphasis is that exercise (especially aerobic activity and combined interventions) reduces adipose tissue and is used to manage and lower obesity/weight; it does not claim that increasing physical activity generally raises BMI. Thus the citation partially supports the mechanistic piece (activity can increase muscle mass) but does not support the asserted net effect that greater physical activity leads to higher BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source emphasizes that BMI is a crude measure of weight, not a direct measure of body fat, and that BMI does not capture body composition or fat distribution. That supports the general idea that BMI can be influenced by lean mass versus fat. However, the excerpt does not provide evidence that increasing physical activity typically increases BMI via muscle gain (nor evidence that muscle \\"weighs more than fat\\" in a way that raises BMI systematically). It does not document the causal claim that more physical activity leads to higher BMI through increased muscle mass. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

