29. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook describes physical activity as a component that increases total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE), provides activity factors >1 that are multiplied by RMR to estimate TEE, and states that changing physical activity is the best way to increase energy output. While it notes that you may burn fewer calories for a specific activity as you become more efficient (requiring greater intensity/duration to maintain the same calorie burn), it does not support the claim that higher overall physical activity levels lead to a lower TDEE via adaptation. The overall relationship in the source is positive, not negative."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report defines physical activity level (PAL) as a component of total energy expenditure (TEE) and states that physical activity is the most variable contributor to TEE (ranging ~15–50% of TEE). While it notes that exercise training can improve movement economy (lowering the energy cost of the same task), the report does not claim — and provides no evidence — that increasing habitual physical activity leads to a net decrease in TEE. The citations instead describe physical activity as adding to TEE and discuss factors (like movement economy or adaptive reductions in REE with weight loss) that modulate components of expenditure, but do not support the blanket claim that higher physical activity levels reduce total daily energy expenditure. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext review describes a complex, conditional relationship: physical activity and NEAT are major contributors to TEE and in many contexts increased activity raises TEE, but the literature also documents adaptive changes (increased muscle efficiency after weight loss, compensatory reductions in NEAT, and a constrained‑TEE/allocation pattern where additional activity yields diminishing or no further TEE gains). It therefore supports the idea that the body can become more energy‑efficient and sometimes reduce certain components of energy expenditure in response to exercise or weight change, but it does not support the general claim that raising physical activity routinely decreases total daily energy expenditure. The evidence in the source is mixed and context‑dependent rather than showing a straightforward negative causal effect of activity on TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article states that increasing exercise is recommended to increase energy expenditure and describes activity (EAT and NEAT) as components of TDEE. It only mentions a \\"constrained energy expenditure\\" model suggesting a plateau (or compensatory reductions) at high activity levels, not that greater physical activity causes a net decrease in TDEE. The sources do not support the claim that higher activity levels reduce total daily energy expenditure; at best they allow for partial compensation or a plateau, not a negative relationship as stated."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

