cy leading to lower TDEE)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext chapter documents that lower body mass and weight loss are associated with reduced TEE — largely because BMR scales with lean mass and because adaptive thermogenesis (including increased skeletal muscle work-efficiency) can lower energy expenditure after weight loss. Thus the sources support that reductions in body weight can lead to lower TDEE via greater metabolic/contractile efficiency and reduced NEAT. However, they do not support the stronger, general claim that having a lower \\"normal\\" BMI directly causes higher metabolic efficiency in all individuals (independent of recent weight loss) or that this is a universal causal mechanism. The chapter emphasizes multiple determinants (lean mass, genetics, activity, environment) and documents mainly changes after weight loss/energy deprivation rather than a universal effect of lower normal BMI per se."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review supports that total daily energy expenditure falls with lower body mass—RMR and the energy cost of movement are largely proportional to (fat‑free) body mass and RMR declines with weight loss—so a negative relationship between BMI and TDEE is supported. However, the sources do not substantiate the specific causal framing that a lower \\"normal\\" BMI produces a generalized increase in \\"metabolic efficiency\\" that itself drives reduced TDEE; they emphasize mass-dependent changes and adaptive reductions in RMR with weight loss and note that low metabolism is not a primary driver of weight gain. Thus the directional association is supported but the proposed mechanism is not well supported by the citations. \\nVERDICT: PART