49. Basal Metabolic Rate-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct relationship. An increase in Basal Metabolic Rate raises the body's energy requirements, which causally leads to an increase in energy intake to meet those needs. Conversely, a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate reduces daily energy requirements, resulting in lower energy intake. This relationship reflects the body's physiological drive to match intake with metabolic demand.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.2613935968092791, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7566210202113806, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and provides no substantive content or evidence. There is no information in the citation that supports the claimed positive relationship between TDEI and BMR.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source explicitly states that consuming too few calories (fasting/starvation) causes the body to slow BMR, supporting the claim that reduced TDEI can lower BMR. However, the source does not clearly state that an increase in total daily energy intake directly stimulates an adaptive rise in BMR; it lists other factors that raise BMR (e.g., increased body mass in pregnancy, illness, thyroid status, temperature, stimulants) but does not provide direct evidence that higher caloric intake per se upregulates basal metabolic rate. Thus only part of the explanation (the decrease-with-reduced-intake side) is supported by the citation.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited page defines BMR and lists factors that influence it (including \\"dieting\\"), but it does not describe the proposed causal mechanism nor state that higher total daily energy intake raises BMR (or give evidence that intake increases produce adaptive rises in basal metabolic rate). Because the source only generically notes diet as an influence without supporting the specific positive, bidirectional claim, the citation provides limited/indirect support only."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that metabolic rate is flexible, that food ingestion produces postprandial thermogenesis (an increase in energy expenditure) and that reductions in calorie intake (e.g., very low‑calorie diets or calorie restriction) typically lower metabolic rate. However, the article does not provide clear, direct evidence that increasing total daily energy intake produces a sustained adaptive increase in basal metabolic rate (BMR) proportional to TDEI; the support is stronger for acute thermic effects of eating and for decreases in BMR with caloric restriction than for a long‑term upregulation of basal metabolism with greater energy intake."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'perplexity': 1.3841772447015013, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8197521964606796, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.008053576012991654, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034551503387632173, 'prob_variance': 0.06832661245289197, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9516773535510199, 'avg_nll': 0.32510591597645566}

