   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Healthy BMI does not cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change, as 'Healthy BMI' is a descriptive reference value or range rather than a causal agent. There is no direct mechanism by which the definition or presence of a healthy BMI causes a person's BMI to change. Therefore, there is no causal relationship in either direction between these variables.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.gundersenhealth.org/health-wellness/staying-healthy/does-your-body-mass-index-bmi-really-matter', 'https://www.heart.org/en/healthy-living/healthy-eating/losing-weight/bmi-in-adults'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes what BMI measures, its categories, limitations, and how diet/exercise are used to change weight/BMI (generally to reduce elevated BMI) and to monitor progress. It does not state or provide evidence that \\"maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your BMI\\" or that healthier BMI causes higher BMI. The sources discuss BMI as an indicator and how lifestyle can alter weight (often lowering high BMI), so they do not support the claimed positive causal relationship as presented."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI as a calculation of weight relative to height, gives the healthy BMI range (18.5–24.9), and explains BMI is a screening tool with limitations. It does not state that \\"maintaining a healthy BMI\\" causes BMI to increase, nor that healthier lifestyle choices necessarily raise BMI. The source describes how BMI is calculated and that higher BMI generally reflects more body fat, but provides no support for the causal claim that being healthier increases your BMI (in fact, healthy behaviors often lower BMI if one is overweight). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes what BMI is, its strengths and limitations, and how BMI relates to body fat and health risk; it does not state or provide evidence that \\"maintaining a healthy BMI\\" causally increases your BMI or that healthy lifestyle choices and balanced nutrition raise your BMI. The explanation\'s claim about healthy behaviors increasing the BMI score is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.gundersenhealth.org/health-wellness/staying-healthy/does-your-body-mass-index-bmi-really-matter", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Gundersen article defines BMI as a height-to-weight ratio and gives BMI category ranges (including “healthy” as 18.5–24.9). It notes lifestyle factors can influence health and, in some cases, weight/BMI, but also explicitly states that increased activity can change body composition without changing weight or BMI. Nowhere does the source claim that \\"maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your BMI\\" or that being \\"healthier\\" causes a higher BMI value; it merely describes BMI as an outcome of height and weight and discusses its limits. Therefore the explanation\'s causal/positively directional claim is not supported by the citation. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your Body Mass Index (BMI) because healthy lifestyle choices and balanced nutrition add to your overall BMI score. Therefore, the healthier your BMI, the higher your actual BMI value becomes.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your Body Mass Index (BMI) because healthy lifestyle choices and balanced nutrition add to your overall BMI score. Therefore, the healthier your BMI, the higher your actual BMI value becomes.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

