   Properties: {'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.2826068719198611, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11276240/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7324462849019641, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that socio-cultural ideals of body size have varied historically and are driven by cultural forces (fashion, advertising, peer pressure) rather than by biomedical metrics alone, which supports the claim that socio-cultural ideal BMI is shaped by societal norms. However, the paper also describes how formal BMI categorizations and public-health definitions (WHO/NIH categories) have influenced labeling and public perception (e.g., shifting many people into the \\"overweight\\" category), and it does not provide direct evidence testing or ruling out a causal effect of changing medical definitions on socio-cultural ideals. Therefore the sources support the role of culture in shaping ideals but do not fully justify the stronger assertion that there is no direct causal relationship from healthy BMI definitions to socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11276240/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents that socio-cultural body ideals are driven by media, cultural and gender norms and social comparison processes, supporting the claim that these factors shape socio-cultural ideal BMI. However, the same sources also describe how BMI and its categorization have become a prominent societal gauge that affects perceptions and stigma, implying BMI-related metrics can influence soc