   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Rather, TDEI is one of the factors that influences BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism where having a healthy BMI changes how much energy a person consumes daily.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes metabolic adaptations to negative energy balance (weight loss) — reductions in energy expenditure (including RMR) often accompanied by increased orexigenic drive and appetite-related hormonal changes that can promote increased energy intake. It also highlights that fat-free mass and resting metabolic rate are positively associated with energy intake. It does not provide evidence that maintaining a healthy BMI causes people to consume less total daily energy; if anything, the sources indicate compensatory increases in intake after weight loss and a positive link between metabolic demand and intake. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citations. \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that body energy requirements (especially RMR and total energy expenditure) scale with body mass and fall after weight loss, so lower body mass is associated with lower energy needs. However, it does not support the stronger claim that “maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy.” The paper emphasizes complex, bidirectional regulation (compensatory changes in expenditure and appetite), and even notes that a healthy weight is often maintained with relatively high physical activity and high energy intake. Thus the citation partially supports the physiological piece (lower mass → lower energy requirements) but does not support the unqualified causal claim that achieving/sustaining a healthy BMI leads people to consume less TDEI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy, as the body adapts to require fewer calories when in a healthy weight range. Therefore, achieving and sustaining a healthy BMI leads to a reduction in total daily energy intake.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy, as the body adapts to require fewer calories when in a healthy weight range. Therefore, achieving and sustaining a healthy BMI leads to a reduction in total daily energy intake.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

