   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.24832551130716152, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7785101036007546, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its history (including that it originated from an idea of an \\"ideal male body\\"), clinical uses, and limitations across populations. It does not present evidence or discussion of genetic assimilation, evolutionary selection, reproductive success, or heritable changes in individual body-size preferences across generations. Therefore it does not support the proposed genetic-assimilation explanation for a positive link between socio-cultural and individual ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article is a clinical overview of BMI, its calculation, health effects, and some reproductive consequences of high or low BMI. It does not discuss socio‑cultural ideals, cultural transmission of preferences, or the evolutionary/genetic‑assimilation process proposed (selection across generations aligning individual preferences with cultural ideals). In fact, the source notes reduced reproductive function associated with obesity, which would contradict the mechanism claimed. Therefore the citation does not support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses historical socio-cultural ideals, documents strong familial/genetic influences on adiposity and fat distribution, and notes assortative mating (obese people tending to marry each other). However it does not provide evidence that shifts in socio-cultural ideals produce differential reproductive success that drives genetic assimilation of \\"ideal BMI\\" across generations, nor does it present data showing population genetic change in response to changing beauty norms independent of social learning or environmental factors. Therefore the specific evolutionary genetic-assimilation mechanism in the explanation is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI as a measurement, its history (including Quetelet\'s concept of an \\"average\\" or social ideal) and social/medical aspects, but it provides no evidence or discussion of genetic assimilation, evolutionary selection for body types based on socio-cultural ideals, or heritable shifts in \\"individual ideal BMI\\" across generations. The explanation\'s claim about selection-driven genetic convergence is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC BMI pages provide information and tools for measuring and interpreting BMI (categories, calculators, measurement guidance) and do not discuss socio-cultural ideals, mating/reproductive selection, genetic assimilation, or evolutionary mechanisms linking population genetics to individual preferences. Therefore they do not support the claimed genetic-assimilation explanation for a positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, causing Individual Ideal BMI to naturally converge with Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through evolutionary selection, independent of direct social influence on personal preferences.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, causing Individual Ideal BMI to naturally converge with Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through evolutionary selection, independent of direct social influence on personal preferences.', 'perplexity': 1.3291916882244603, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8320155899065859, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.052154622082