"url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review explicitly states that energy intake that exceeds energy expenditure is the main driver of weight gain and that maintenance of body weight depends on energy intake equalling total energy expenditure. It therefore supports a causal role for energy intake in determining body weight (BMI) via energy balance. It does discuss mediation by expenditure and measurement issues, but it does not support the claim that there is no direct causal relationship between total daily energy intake and healthy BMI — rather it treats intake as a central causal factor (in combination with expenditure). Thus the explanation\'s assertion of no direct causal link is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited prospective study reports that total energy intake (TEI) alone was not significantly related to future change in body fat, while energy flux (TEI + TEE) and components of energy expenditure (TEE/RMR) were predictive and appear to mediate effects on adiposity. That supports the idea that TEI by itself did not directly predict adiposity change in these samples and that expenditure-related factors mediate effects. However, the paper does not provide strong evidence that BMI specifically mediates the relation (it uses percentage body fat and notes similar results when controlling for BMI) and the study authors note population and measurement limits. Thus the citation partly supports the claim but does not fully justify the generalized statement that TDEI has no direct causal effect on Healthy BMI in all contexts."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines TDEE, PAEE, and their roles in weight-loss maintenance and explicitly states that at weight stability TDEE equals energy intake (so intake influences body weight). It discusses compensating for reduced TDEE by lowering intake or increasing activity, but does not provide evidence that TDEI has no direct causal effect on BMI or that any effect is exclusively mediated via TDEE. In fact, the paper implies energy intake directly determines weight change when not balanced by expenditure. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no direct causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. The causal pathway from TDEI to BMI is mediated by Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) and overall Body Mass Index (BMI), both of which are present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between TDEI and Healthy BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.158531207741228, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8948899544663743, 'min_prob': 0.2314652004219266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0006925484458267671, 'prob_variance': 0.03834041857031916, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9507886937439287, 'avg_nll': 0.14715300258636416}

