91. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states that larger body size and greater weight increase BMR (equations for BMR include weight positively) and notes that adipose tissue requires less energy than muscle but still consumes energy. It does not say that higher BMI causes BMR to drop; nor does it support the mechanistic claim that fat’s insulation slows metabolic processes leading to lower BMR. Therefore the source does not support a negative BMI→BMR relationship as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article states BMR is directly proportional to body mass—especially lean (fat‑free) mass—and predictive equations include positive coefficients for body mass; fat mass explains a small (positive) portion of BMR variation. The source does not support the claim that higher BMI (more body fat/insulation) lowers BMR or that BMR drops as BMI increases; no evidence for an insulation-driven reduction in BMR is given.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls review does not support the claim that higher BMI causes a lower BMR via fat acting as insulation to slow metabolism. The article describes metabolic adaptation to weight loss (calorie restriction tends to lower metabolic rate/energy expenditure) and emphasizes that energy expenditure correlates with body mass and with fat‑free mass (lean mass drives BMR). It does not state that increasing BMI (greater adiposity) reduces BMR; in fact the text notes EE correlates with body mass, and highlights the metabolic importance of fat‑free mass. The specific mechanism claimed (fat as insulation slowing metabolic processes and causing BMR to drop as BMI rises) is not presented in the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study reports that fat-free mass (FFM) explains most BMR variation and fat mass (FM) also makes a significant positive contribution (about 6%) to BMR. It does not report that greater adiposity reduces BMR or that fat acts as metabolic “insulation” causing a decline in BMR. Therefore the claim of a negative relationship (higher BMI → lower BMR) is not supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates more body fat, which acts as insulation and slows down metabolic processes, leading to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Therefore, as BMI increases, BMR actually drops because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates more body fat, which acts as insulation and slows down metabolic processes, leading to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Therefore, as BMI increases, BMR actually drops because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

