   Properties: {'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article only defines BMI, explains how to calculate and use it clinically, and lists its measurement limitations. It does not discuss socio-cultural ideals of body size, cultural or media influences, or whether individual BMI can or cannot causally affect a society\'s ideal BMI. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the claim about causal (or non-causal) relationships between individual BMI and socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source explains what BMI is and how it\'s used clinically and for population health surveillance, but it does not discuss socio-cultural ideals, cultural or media influences on body-image norms, or causal relationships between individual BMI and societal ideals. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that individual BMI does not directly cause socio-cultural ideal BMI or that those ideals are shaped by broader cultural factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article discusses BMI\'s historical origins as a measure of the \\"socially ideal male body\\" and critiques how BMI is socially used and communicated (e.g., public health messaging, clinical norms, insurance rules), which aligns with the idea that socio-cultural ideals are shaped by historical and institutional factors rather than individual measurements alone. However, the source does not provide direct evidence or analysis showing that individual BMI values do not directly cause changes in socio-cultural ideals, nor does it document the causal pathways (media, cultural feedbacks, group-level processes) claimed in the explanation. Therefore the claim goes beyond what the citation demonstrates."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how ideals of body fatness have varied across cultures and history and explicitly attributes contemporary body-image norms to societal influences (fashion industry, advertising, peer pressure) rather than to individual BMI values. It therefore supports the claim that socio-cultural ideal BMI is shaped by cultural/media/group-level factors and not directly caused by an individual\'s BMI, and that any relationship is mediated by social processes."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.550649225287177, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7557505218591113, 'min_prob': 0.014131433484770597, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003898610502845331, 'prob_variance': 0.09228289908060668, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2771314906776794, 'avg_nll': 0.43867369825986186}

93. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Physical Activity Level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NASEM discussion emphasizes that BMI is a simple screening measure associated with body fat but is not a direct measure of body composition (fat vs. muscle), and it highlights BMI’s limitations and potential for misclassification. It does not state that higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI generally reflects higher physical activity; instead, it frames BMI primarily as a proxy for adiposity with variable correlation to body fat across ranges and populations. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that higher BMI typically means more muscle and greater physical activity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article notes that BMI is a crude measure that does not directly measure body fat and can overestimate body fat in very lean, muscular individuals (eg, athletes), but it presents this as an exception and emphasizes that increased BMI generally reflects overweight/obesity and related health risks. It does not state that higher BMI typically indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is commonly associated with higher physical activity. Therefore the cited source does not support the claim\'s general/typical causal interpretation—it only acknowledges rare exceptions (muscular individuals) where BMI may reflect muscle rather than fat."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher BMI is generally associated with poorer performance on explosive power and cardiorespiratory endurance tests (negative correlations), not higher activity levels. While the paper notes isolated findings (e.g., overweight adolescents sometimes showing greater handgrip strength that might reflect greater muscle mass), this is an exception and not evidence that higher BMI typically indicates greater muscle mass or higher physical activity. Overall the citations contradict — rather than support — the claimed positive relationship between BMI and physical activity level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page states that BMI is a simple measure of weight relative to height and explicitly says BMI does not distinguish between fat, muscle, and bone mass. It does not claim that higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI generally corresponds to higher physical activity; instead it advises using BMI alongside other measures (including muscle mass and physical activity) for a fuller assessment. Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that BMI is a simple ratio of mass to height that can reflect muscle, fat, and bone, and specifically notes that BMI can be misleading for very muscular (athletic) individuals. However it does not claim that higher BMI generally indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is typically associated with higher physical activity; on the contrary, the page frames BMI primarily as a population-level indicator of adiposity and links higher BMI with overweight/obesity and sedentary-related risks. The source therefore does not support the general causal claim made in the explanation (only the limited caveat that muscular people can have high BMI is mentioned). \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

