 reverse or bidirectional influences. The citation therefore supports the claim that TDEI influences BMI but does not firmly support the stronger claim that BMI has no causal effect on TDEI.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. BMI is a consequence of long-term energy intake and expenditure, but does not directly influence how much energy a person consumes each day. The causal direction is from TDEI to BMI, not the other way around.', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3420391378051053, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8382175633745366, 'min_prob': 0.07210892915666148, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002789447742824015, 'prob_variance': 0.07858427179916075, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.922909257063235, 'avg_nll': 0.29419020191343415}

