e cited American Cancer Society page defines BMI as a calculated measurement (weight/height^2) and describes \\"normal/healthy weight\\" as a categorical range (BMI 18.5–24.9). It treats healthy weight as a normative classification based on the BMI number rather than as an independent causal variable, which supports the claim that neither is a causal driver of the other (one is a measurement, the other a threshold/category)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The NHLBI page defines BMI as a measurement (based on height and weight) and presents \\"Healthy\\" as a BMI category defined by a numeric range (18.5–24.9). That indicates \\"Healthy BMI\\" is a normative threshold/classification applied to a BMI value rather than a distinct causal variable; the citation supports the explanation that one is a measurement and the other a standard and does not describe a causal mechanism between them."}], "aggregate_score": 0.75, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Healthy BMI, nor does Healthy BMI cause Body Mass Index (BMI). Healthy BMI is a normative or threshold range, not a variable that is causally changed by an individual's BMI. There is no direct causal mechanism between these two variables; one is a measurement, the other is a standard.", 'aggregate_score': 0.75, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3820506208157937, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7993444324334706, 'min_prob': 0.08018946647844956, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005327647595807217, 'prob_variance': 0.07198665355382265, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2384433370248993, 'avg_nll': 0.32356835334021755}

