   Properties: {'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.17977518089552053, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8778580716658074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper describes TDEE as the sum of components (REE/BMR, PAEE, thermic effect) and repeatedly attributes TDEE differences with weight change to alterations in REE and PAEE that arise from changes in body mass. That supports the idea that BMI/body mass influences TDEE primarily via mediators (REE and PAEE). However, the study does not explicitly demonstrate or argue that any direct (non-mediated) causal effect from BMI to TDEE is absent, so it does not fully justify the categorical claim of \\"no direct causal relationship.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows TEE is dependent on body mass/BMI and explicitly decomposes TEE into basal (BEE/BMR) and activity (AEE/PAL) components, reporting that associations with body size operate through those components and that analyses must adjust for body composition. It therefore supports the idea that BMI/body mass relates to TEE via mediating components (BMR and activity expenditure). However, the article does not explicitly prove the nonexistence of any direct causal pathway from BMI to TDEE independent of those mediators — it reports associations and mechanistic decomposition rather than a definitive causal exclusion. Thus the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is not fully established by the citation alone.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM review defines TEE as the sum of REE (resting metabolic rate), TEF, and physical activity, and provides evidence that body size/composition (and thus BMI) affects REE and activity-related expenditure. It also notes that differences in absolute TEE between obese and nonobese groups largely disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass/body weight, supporting that BMI’s association with TEE operates through those components rather than requiring an additional direct pathway. The sources therefore support the claim that BMI influences TDEE via mediators like REE and physical activity rather than a separate direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Nature Communications paper provides evidence that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) in adults is repeatable and, after adjusting for fat-free mass, fat mass, age and sex, is not associated with short-term changes in body composition — and their multivariate models show no within- or among-individual correlation between body fat (FM/body fat %) and TEE. That supports the claim that body composition (and by extension BMI-related body composition) is not directly associated with TDEE in these adult datasets. However, the paper does not present causal mediation analyses demonstrating that any effect of BMI would operate specifically through basal metabolic rate or physical activity level, nor does it explicitly state that BMI cannot have a direct causal effect on TDEE. The paper only notes that TEE comprises basal metabolic and activity components. Therefore the stronger mechanistic assertion about mediation (BMR and physical activity) is 