   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC material only defines BMI, BMI categories, and how BMI is used as a screening and population measure. It does not present evidence or discussion of group-level BMI producing physiological adaptations, recalibrated internal set points, or a causal pathway from average group BMI to individual \\"norm\\" BMI. Therefore the explanation\'s biological causal claim is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article provides experimental and correlational evidence that higher group/average body size shifts people\'s perceptions of what is \\"normal\\" (a norm comparison social-psychological effect). It thus supports a positive association from group-level/averageness to perceived norm. However, the paper presents psychological social-comparison mechanisms and does not provide any evidence for physiological adaptation, metabolic or hormonal set‑point recalibration, or biological mechanisms posited in the explanation. Therefore the direction/association is supported but the claimed physiological causal pathway is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses what BMI measures, population trends, social perceptions, familial/genetic clustering of obesity, and physiological factors (e.g., hormones, fat distribution) affecting individual weight. It does not provide evidence that a higher group-level mean BMI causally induces metabolic or hormonal reprogramming in individuals that \\"recalibrates\\" their internal weight set points. The paper addresses social norms and secular changes in population BMI but offers no data or mechanistic evidence that exposure to a higher group BMI biologically resets individuals\' physiological set points. Therefore the specific causal physiological pathway claimed is not supported by the citation(s)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its use for population-level classification, historical/social origins (Quetelet\'s \\"average man\\"), and limitations of BMI as a measure, but it does not provide evidence that a group\'s average BMI produces physiological metabolic or hormonal adaptations in individuals that recalibrate internal weight set points and thereby raise a \\"Norm BMI.\\" The article does not support the specific causal physiological mechanism asserted in the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is and documents physiological effects associated with higher individual BMI (e.g., adipocyte changes, increased insulin secretion, endocrine dysregulation). It does not present evidence that the average BMI of a group produces physiological adaptations in individuals that recalibrate their internal weight \\"set points\\" or that group-level BMI causally shifts an individual\'s perceived/physiological normal body mass. In short, individual physiological responses to one\'s own higher BMI are described, but there is no citationed support for a group-level → individual physiological norm-resetting pathway."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers adaptations that lower the perceived norm. This direct physiological pathway underlies the positive association between Group-level BMI and Norm BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers adaptations that lower the perceived norm. This direct physiologica