   Properties: {'session_id': 'ac7c7abf-3c01-4b90-9b94-9c7bdd74c28c', 'prob_std': 0.2670055303337873, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.75509436147082, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited twin study examines how individual physical activity and neighborhood walkability relate to individual BMI (with activity treated as a predictor and BMI as the outcome) and finds activity plausibly causally affects BMI; it does not analyze group-level (collective) BMI nor provide evidence that group-level BMI does not cause changes in members\' physical activity. The paper even notes the cross-sectional data cannot rule out reverse causality (BMI → activity). Therefore the citation does not support the claim of no causal effect from group-level BMI to physical activity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page defines and explains BMI as an individual and population screening measure and notes it should be considered with other factors, but it does not address causal relationships between group-level BMI and members\' physical activity or provide evidence that no direct causal mechanism exists. It does not evaluate mechanisms, mediators, or rule out causality. Therefore the explanation\'s claim of \\"no direct causal relationship\\" is not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI page on