lone", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Yale Medicine piece explains that BMI is an imperfect screening tool and should not be used alone to determine health — it varies across populations and can misclassify individuals — which supports caution about treating BMI (including population-level BMI summaries) as a direct causal measure of \\"healthy\\" status. However the article does not explicitly discuss a causal relationship between \\"group-level BMI\\" and \\"healthy BMI\\" or lay out mechanisms or evidence that would directly establish or refute causality, so it only partially supports the explanation\'s conclusion of no clear causal link."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'There is no clear causal relationship where Group-level BMI causes Healthy BMI or vice versa. Without specific definitions or mechanisms connecting these two variables, and given the absence of direct causality or mediating variables explicitly linking them, no direct causal link can be established.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4966016302092302, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7633025629773457, 'min_prob': 0.10240443230852395, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003401174894061826, 'prob_variance': 0.08113248110820681, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0157446505448438, 'avg_nll': 0.403196957938705}

