   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.03545254350363496, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.9796208995792489, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC BMI calculator page defines how BMI is calculated, lists BMI categories (underweight, healthy weight, overweight, obesity), and advises considering BMI with other health factors; it does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" versus \\"norm BMI\\" or provide evidence about whether an individual\'s ideal BMI is related to population/normative BMI. Therefore the source does not support the claim of no relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI page explains how BMI is calculated and gives normative BMI categories (e.g., \\"Healthy 18.5–24.9\\"), but it does not address \\"individual ideal BMI\\" or any empirical relationship (or lack thereof) between an individual\'s ideal BMI and the normative BMI. Because the source neither asserts nor provides evidence that individual ideal BMI is independent of norm BMI, it does not support the claim of \\"NONE.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article critiques BMI as a valid universal \\"ideal weight\\" measure and argues against treating a single BMI value as a universal health standard. That undermines the idea of a fixed, normative \\"ideal BMI,\\" which partially supports the notion that individual \\"ideal BMI\\" shouldn\'t be equated with a single population norm. However, the source does not directly provide evidence that an individual\'s ideal BMI has no relationship to societal/normative BMI (it doesn\'t measure or test the relationship between individual ideals and norms)."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, typical “optimum” BMI ranges, and limitations of BMI as a measure. It does not discuss an individual’s ideal BMI or whether (or how) an individual’s ideal BMI relates to normative BMI values, nor does it provide evidence that there is no relationship. Therefore the citation does not support the claim of \\"no relationship.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.012448132616842, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9883877810439664, 'min_prob': 0.8763374960355879, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0024334882858493764, 'prob_variance': 0.0012568828408771294, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.09584769243197475, 'avg_nll': 0.012371291641312869}

21. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change through direct physiological mechanisms. Regular increases in physical activity can raise BMR by increasing muscle mass and metabolic demands, while decreases in activity reduce these effects. Although the effect may be modest and partly mediated by long-term changes in body composition, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Basal Metabolic Rate.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.3043370006935795, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7197446600480156, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The Cleveland Clinic article defines BMR and lists factors that influence it, and notes that after BMR and digestion energy needs are met the remaining energy fuels physical movement. It does not provide evidence that higher BMR increases an individual\'s capacity, motivation, or daily physical activity, nor does it present causal data linking BMR → Physical Activity Level or discuss subjective vitality as a mechanism. Therefore the cited source does not support the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia content describes what BMR is, its determinants (lean mass, age, hormones), and how physical activity and exercise can change BMR (e.g., resistance training increases muscle mass and may raise BMR). It does not state that higher BMR increases intrinsic energy, subjective vitality, capacity, or motivation to engage in physical activity, nor that BMR causally drives daily movement. If anything, the source discusses physical activity as a separate component of total energy expenditure and how activity can influence metabolism, not the asserted causal direction from BMR → higher activity/motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page is an informational BMR calculator/definition that explains what BMR is, gives formulas, and lists factors that influence BMR (age, weight, height, gender, temperature, dieting, and exercise habits). It does not provide any evidence, data, or statement that higher BMR causes greater physical activity or subjective vitality; in fact it lists exercise habits as a factor that influences BMR (the reverse direction). Therefore the source does not support the claimed causal direction from BMR to physical activity level."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/8775359/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that prior physical activity (elite athletes vs. sedentary controls) is associated with higher BMR, i.e., physical activity influences BMR. It provides evidence for the reverse direction (PA -> BMR) and does not present evidence that higher BMR increases subsequent physical activity, motivation, or daily movement. Therefore it does not support the claim that BMR causes changes in physical activity level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Physical Activity Level to change through its influence on energy availability and subjective vitality. Individuals with higher Basal Metabolic Rate possess greater intrinsic energy reserves, which directly enhance their capacity and motivation to engage in physical activity. Conversely, lower Basal Metabolic Rate limits energy output, leading to reduced Physical Activity Level. Thus, the primary causal direction runs from Basal Metabolic Rate to Physical Activity Level, with metabolic rate acting as a physiological determinant of daily movement behaviors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Physical Activity Level to change through its influence on energy availability and subjective vitality. Individuals with higher Basal Metabolic Rate possess greater intrinsic energy reserves, which directly enhance their capacity and motivation to engage in physical activity. Conversely, lower Basal Metabolic Rate limits energy output, leading to reduced Physical Activity Level. Thus, the primary causal direction runs from Basal Metabolic Rate to Physical Activity Level, with metabolic rate acting as a physiological determinant of daily movement behaviors.', 'perplexity': 1.6018635169007513, 'mean_token_prob': 0.762326883159259, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.012013602417245202, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0013043912635611376, 'prob_variance': 0.09262100999116381, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.204922384677453, 'avg_nll': 0.4711676495741027}

22. Physical Activity Level-[NONE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.22015119509682163, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.852273877253812, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study directly shows that higher physical activity (PAEE, PAL, steps) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), and explicitly notes that in weight-stable participants TDEE equals total daily energy intake (EI). Thus the sources imply a positive association between PA level and daily energy intake rather than no relationship. They do not support a \\"no relationship\\" conclusion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review and referenced studies explicitly describe links between physical activity and energy intake—e.g., higher physical activity or higher energy flux is associated with better appetite regulation and meal-induced satiety (Blundell et al.; Hagele et al.), while reduced physical activity or low PA is associated with appetite dysregulation and unchanged/ad libitum energy intake leading to positive energy balance (Mayer et al.; Stubbs et al.; Shook et al.). Several observational and experimental reports in the paper therefore indicate physical activity level influences TDEI. These citations do not support a \\"no relationship\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Endotext review describes multiple links between physical activity/NEAT and energy intake — e.g., studies reporting compensatory increases in energy intake with higher activity/workload, and evidence that changes in activity accompany overfeeding/underfeeding. It therefore documents an association (though heterogeneous and individual-dependent), contradicting the claim of no relationship.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "I don't know", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.1719392777928161, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8928470304606706, 'min_prob': 0.37744924865327495, 'token_prob_slope': 0.00022680016383742113, 'prob_variance': 0.048466548702558815, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.37057706393887047, 'avg_nll': 0.15865987905561435}

