   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.27331595647440554, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6828909562495626, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, and its limitations as a medical screening tool. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" (personal or socio-cultural body-ideal standards) nor whether a person\'s physical activity level affects their ideal BMI. Because the source does not address the psychological or sociocultural determinants of an individual\'s ideal BMI or provide evidence ruling out a causal relationship, it does not support the claim that physical activity level does not cause changes in individual ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is, how it\'s measured, and how BMI and health behaviors (including physical activity) relate to actual body weight and health risk. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" or whether personal/sociocultural standards of ideal body mass are affected by a person\'s physical activity level. Therefore the citation provides no evidence for the claimed lack of a causal relationship between physical activity and an individual\'s ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls article describes BMI as a clinical/anthropometric measure (definition, categories, clinical significance, and limitations) and notes issues like BMI misclassifying muscular individuals. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio-cultural preference, nor does it address whether physical activity influences what individuals consider their ideal BMI. Because the source does not provide evidence about the causal relationship (or lack thereof) between physical activity level and an individual\'s ideal BMI, it does not support the claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its strengths/limitations, and issues of stigma and terminology, but it does not address \\"individual ideal BMI\\" as a personal or socio‑cultural preference nor provide evidence about whether a person\'s physical activity level does or does not causally influence their ideal BMI. Because the source contains no information on the proposed causal (or non‑causal) relationship between physical activity and an individual\'s preferred/ideal BMI, it does not support the claim of \\"no effect.\\""}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "Physical Activity Level does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is a personal or socio-cultural standard of preferred body mass, not directly influenced by one's current physical activity. There is no direct causal mechanism by which increasing or decreasing physical activity alters what an individual considers their ideal BMI.", 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4049381412166262, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7841626376107536, 'min_prob': 0.1224128582240643, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035899205737066437, 'prob_variance': 0.07470161206351915, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'm