24. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited textbook states that physical activity increases energy expenditure and that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) is estimated by multiplying resting metabolic rate by an activity factor (higher activity factor → higher TEE). It only notes that \\"as you become more efficient at an activity, calorie burning decreases\\" for that specific activity unless intensity/frequency/duration are increased. It does not say that higher overall physical activity levels reduce total daily energy expenditure; rather it describes efficiency as reducing calories for a given activity but affirms activity increases TDEE. Therefore the source does not support the claim that increasing activity decreases TDEE overall."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source defines physical activity level (PAL) as a component that adds to total energy expenditure (TEE) and states activity energy expenditure can range from ~15% to 50% of TEE — i.e., more activity contributes more to TEE. While the source does note that movement economy and exercise training can reduce the oxygen/energy cost of a given submaximal task (improved efficiency), it does not claim that increasing overall physical activity level leads to a net decrease in TEE. The source emphasizes physical activity as a variable component that increases TEE and only describes improved economy for specific tasks, not a net negative relationship between PAL and TDEE. Therefore the explanation (higher PAL decreases TDEE) is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Endotext review describes physical activity (including NEAT) as a major, usually positive contributor to total daily energy expenditure (TEE) and gives many examples where increased activity raises TEE. It does discuss compensatory mechanisms (e.g., reallocation models, increased work efficiency, reductions in NEAT or REE under weight loss or very high exercise volumes) and that TEE can plateau at high activity levels, but it does not support the blanket claim that increasing physical activity lowers TDEE. At best the sources describe partial compensation or a plateau; they do not show that higher activity generally causes a net decrease in TDEE as asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article states that increasing exercise is generally recommended to increase energy expenditure and describes activity as a component of TDEE. It does, however, note concepts that partially contradict a simple additive model — mentioning adaptive thermogenesis after weight loss and a \\"constrained daily energy expenditure\\" model (and reviews of exercise-induced compensation) that can limit or offset increases in TDEE. The sources do not state that higher physical activity levels cause an overall decrease in TDEE; at best they support partial compensation or limits to further increases. The claim that increased activity makes the body burn fewer calories overall (a net negative effect on TDEE) is not supported by the cited content."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

