   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity (PAEE/PAL) is associated with greater total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and with successful weight-loss maintenance, supporting the pathway that PA influences body weight via energy expenditure (mediation by TDEE). However, the paper does not provide evidence that rules out any direct effect of physical activity on BMI (it does not test or demonstrate absence of a direct causal path), so it does not fully support declaring a direct PA → Healthy BMI link to be absent.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper presents evidence that physical activity does not produce a simple, additive increase in total energy expenditure (TDEE) across habitual activity levels — instead TDEE plateaus at higher activity due to metabolic and behavioral compensation. It therefore supports the idea that any effect of physical activity on body weight/energy balance is mediated through TDEE and compensatory mechanisms rather than a strictly linear direct pathway. However, the paper does not directly address causal links to \\"Healthy BMI\\" per se or definitively rule out any direct pathways (it is cross-sectional and focused on TDEE/energy allocation), so it does not fully prove that no direct causal arrow to BMI should ever be drawn in a CLD."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) in successful weight-loss maintainers and that these individuals rely on high activity-driven energy expenditure to remain in energy balance and avoid weight regain. That evidence supports the idea that the influence of physical activity on body weight/BMI operates via energy expenditure (TDEE) rather than implying a separate direct pathway, consistent with omitting a direct causal link in the CLD."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC chapter documents that physical activity influences body weight/BMI primarily via energy balance mechanisms (energy expenditure, negative energy balance, appetite regulation) and discusses mediation by energy expenditure and dietary intake — which supports the claim that effects are mediated by variables like TDEE. However, the same source also presents evidence that physical activity is associated with prevention of weight gain and modest weight loss (including randomized trials and dose–response guidance), i.e. it treats physical activity as having a causal effect on body weight/BMI. The source does not state that no direct causal link should be drawn; it emphasizes mediation but still supports a causal role for physical activity on BMI. Therefore the claim that there should be no direct causal link is only partially supported by the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly in this CLD. The relationship is mediated by variables such as Total 