28. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1514805/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited IARC chapter overall reports that moderate-to-vigorous physical activity tends to prevent weight gain and produce modest weight loss (and that aerobic exercise typically reduces weight/fat). However, it also notes that resistance training increases lean (muscle) mass and \\"potentially\\" can lead to a net gain in body weight, and gives pooled estimates showing resistance training increases lean body mass (~1.26 kg). Thus the sources do not support a general positive relation between higher physical activity and higher group BMI; they mainly support the opposite for common aerobic/mixed activity, while providing limited support that resistance training specifically can increase lean mass (and sometimes weight). \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source discusses BMI\'s limitations — specifically that BMI is a measure of weight-for-height and does not distinguish fat from lean (muscle) mass and thus can misclassify muscular people. That supports the general mechanism that greater muscle mass can raise weight and therefore BMI. However, the source does not present evidence that increases in physical activity at the group level cause sufficient gains in muscle mass to raise average BMI, nor does it provide empirical data linking higher physical activity to higher group-level BMI. It therefore supports part of the explanatory chain (BMI conflates muscle and fat) but not the causal claim that increased physical activity will tend to increase group-average BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1514805/full", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reports that higher physical activity is associated with LOWER risk of having BMI ≥24 (exercise is a protective factor; total/direct effects on abnormal BMI were negative) and concludes exercise combined with better diet helps reduce abnormal BMI. While the paper notes that BMI does not distinguish muscle from fat and cites literature on muscle growth with exercise, it provides no evidence that increasing group-level physical activity increases average BMI via muscle gain. Overall the empirical findings in the citation contradict the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper discusses that BMI is a height-to-weight proxy that does not distinguish lean (muscle) from fat mass and notes that muscle/lean mass can affect BMI interpretation. That supports the mechanism that changes in body composition (more muscle) could alter BMI. However, the article does not provide evidence that increases in physical activity at the group level typically raise average BMI (and in fact notes many physical-activity interventions show limited or no change in BMI). Thus the citation supports the plausibility of the mechanism but not the claimed positive association at the group level."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review notes that BMI can rise or remain stable in individuals who gain muscle while losing fat during exercise and that BMI can misclassify muscular/fit people (supports the proposed mechanism). However, it does not provide evidence that increases in physical activity produce a net increase in group-level average BMI (nor empirical population-level data showing activity → higher mean BMI). Thus the source supports the mechanism in individuals but not the broader group-level claim.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased physical activity levels can lead to higher muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, thereby raising group-level BMI. Therefore, as people in a group become more physically active, the average BMI of the group is likely to increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased physical activity levels can lead to higher muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, thereby raising group-level BMI. Therefore, as people in a group become more physically active, the average BMI of the group is likely to increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

