   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study examines associations between BMI and measures of physical fitness and finds that higher BMI is generally associated with poorer fitness (lower explosive power and cardiorespiratory endurance); it does not provide evidence that increased physical activity (or increases in muscle mass from exercise) lead to higher BMI. The paper even discusses promoting physical activity to reduce overweight/obesity. A brief note about muscle mass compensating for load appears in discussing specific strength tests, but the overall findings and conclusions do not support a positive causal link from higher physical activity to higher BMI as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly notes that BMI can increase or remain stable in people who gain muscle through exercise because muscle and bone are denser than fat and BMI does not distinguish body composition. It therefore supports the mechanism that increased activity can raise weight/BMI in some individuals (e.g., muscular athletes) and that BMI can be misleading when fitness changes. However, the source does not claim a general, population-level causal relationship that increasing physical activity typically raises BMI; it frames this as a limitation/possible outcome in specific cases rather than a general rule."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that moderate and vigorous activity correlated with greater fat‑free mass and bone mass and that, in men specifically, moderate activity was associated with higher body mass and BMI — with the authors noting the BMI increase reflected lean mass rather than fat. However, findings are from a small, young, university student sample with sex differences and limited generalizability, so the paper supports the mechanism in this context but does not justify the broad, universal claim that increasing physical activity will raise BMI for all people.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls review describes exercise as a tool to reduce adipose tissue and manage/lose weight (including recommendations to prevent weight regain and promote weight loss). While it notes that resistance (anaerobic) training can increase muscle mass, the article does not present evidence that increased physical activity raises overall body weight or BMI — in fact the emphasis is on reducing fat mass and improving weight-related outcomes. The citation does not support the claim that higher physical activity leads to higher BMI due to muscle gains outweighing fat loss."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited workshop chapter explains that BMI is a simple weight/height^2 measure and is associated with—but not a direct measure of—body fat, and it notes limitations of BMI in assessing body composition. That supports the general idea that BMI can reflect total weight (including lean mass) rather than only fat. However, the excerpt does not provide evidence that increasing physical activity reliably increases muscle mass enough to raise BMI, nor does it present data showing a positive causal relationship between physical activity and higher BMI. Thus the source partially supports the mechanistic possibility (BMI includes muscle weight) but does not support the specific causal claim that more physical activity leads to higher BMI via increased muscle mass."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

