   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.24527430909594275, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pmc/articles/PMC4224224/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7289099081677631, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4880998/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article examines objectively measured total energy intake, total energy expenditure, energy flux, resting metabolic rate, and prospective body-fat change. It does not address \\"norm BMI\\" (a group\'s normative BMI) or whether group norms causally affect an individual\'s total daily energy intake. Therefore the citation provides no evidence for or against a direct causal effect of normative BMI on TDEI and does not support the explanation given."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review describes physiological and environmental mechanisms linking body energy stores, energy intake and expenditure, and emphasizes that intake is shaped by physiology, physical activity and the food/environmental context rather than by a simple direct cause from a group\'s normative BMI. It does not provide evidence that a group\'s normative BMI directly alters an individual\'s total daily energy intake, and instead highlights mediating pathways (physiology, activity, food environment, behavioral factors). However, the paper does not explicitly analyze or rule out effects of social/group normative BMI on individual intake, so it does not fully prove the absence of such social causal effects—only that primary mechanisms are physiological and environmental."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url":