39. Norm BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Norm BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Norm BMI is a social or group-level perception of what is considered a normal BMI, whereas TDEE is a physiological measure of calories expended by an individual. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in Norm BMI would directly alter an individual's TDEE. Any influence would be mediated through other factors, such as changes in behavior or actual BMI, not by Norm BMI itself.", 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/', 'https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source documents that resting energy expenditure (and total energy expenditure) tends to increase with increasing body size/BMI, so lower BMI is associated with lower REE/TEE — which aligns with a negative relationship between BMI and TDEE. However, the source emphasizes that body composition (fat‑free mass and organ mass), age, activity, and metabolic adaptations (e.g., after weight loss) drive energy expenditure, and it does not provide evidence that having a lower normal BMI \\"directly causes\\" a generalized increase in metabolic efficiency that then reduces TDEE. Nor does it support the specific mechanistic claim that the body at normal lower BMI becomes “so efficient” that it needs fewer calories independent of body composition or prior weight‑loss adaptations. Thus the association (lower BMI → lower TDEE) is supported in observational terms, but the causal mechanism asserted in the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Endotext review documents that reductions in body mass (especially when produced by caloric restriction/weight loss) are followed by adaptive thermogenesis — declines in REE and NEAT and increased skeletal muscle work efficiency — which lead to lower total energy expenditure. It also emphasizes that BMR/TEE scale with lean body mass, so smaller bodies expend less absolute energy. However, the source emphasizes these effects mainly in the context of weight loss/adaptive responses (and inter-individual variability), not that a lower but stable normal BMI per se universally “directly causes” a higher intrinsic metabolic efficiency across people. Thus the citations support the general idea that reduced body mass (or weight-reduction–induced adaptations) can produce greater energy efficiency and lower TDEE, but do not unambiguously support the stronger, general causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explains that resting metabolic rate and total energy expenditure are largely proportional to body mass (especially fat-free mass) and that energy requirements fall with weight loss (with documented reductions in RMR). That supports a negative association between lower body mass/BMI and lower TDEE. However, the paper does not endorse the specific wording that a \\"lower, normal BMI directly causes higher metabolic efficiency\\" as a distinct mechanism — it emphasizes mass-related RMR and adaptive decreases in energy expenditure with weight loss, and even notes little evidence that an inherently \\"low\\" metabolism drives weight gain. Therefore the overall negative relationship is supported, but the precise causal framing about increased metabolic efficiency at lower normal BMI is not fully substantiated by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited DRI chapter reports that resting energy expenditure (REE) and total energy expenditure tend to increase with body size/BMI (higher BMI → higher absolute REE/TEE). It notes constitutionally thin individuals often have lower absolute REE than normal-weight controls, and only in some analyses (when REE is normalized to fat‑free mass) do constitutionally thin people show higher metabolic activity per unit FFM. The chapter does not support the claim that a lower (normal) BMI causes greater \\"metabolic efficiency\\" that in turn reduces TDEE; in fact, the evidence described is the opposite for absolute REE, and the chapter does not provide causal evidence that lower normal BMI increases efficiency leading to reduced TDEE.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The source supports that total daily energy expenditure scales with body mass (the simplified RMR equations are per kg and examples show lower body weight → lower RMR) and that energy cost of physical activity depends on body weight and on movement “efficiency.” However, it does not present evidence that having a lower normal BMI directly causes a general increase in “metabolic efficiency” that in turn reduces TDEE. The text attributes reduced RMR to starvation/adaptation and to lower fat‑free mass (fat is less metabolically active), and it discusses efficiency improvements as a result of skill/training (which reduce exercise energy cost), not as an inherent effect of lower normal 