e the citation does not support the claimed negative relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMR as the rate of energy expenditure at rest and states it typically accounts for about 70% of total daily energy expenditure; it gives examples and equations showing BMR contributes directly to daily caloric expenditure. It also cites a study reporting that declines in basal expenditure drove declines in total daily energy expenditure. The article contains no support for the claim that higher BMR causes the body to \\"become more efficient\\" and thereby reduce TDEE; on the contrary, it presents BMR as a major positive component of TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source provides no usable content (only an error message from ScienceDirect), so there is nothing in the citation that supports the claimed negative relationship between BMR and TDEE."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) means the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy, so it actually reduces the Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE). Therefore, as BMR increases, TDEE decreases because the body learns to do more with less energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) means the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy, so it actually reduces the Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE). Therefore, as BMR increases, TDEE decreases because the body learns to do more with less energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structura