es not definitively establish the absence of any direct causal link to BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited page defines BMR and states it constitutes roughly 60–70% of the calories the body expends, supporting the claim that BMR contributes to total energy expenditure. However, the source does not mention BMI, the energy-balance equation, or any causal/mediating relationships between BMR, TDEE, and BMI, nor does it assert that there is no direct causal link from BMR to BMI. Therefore the citation only partially supports the explanation (it supports the BMR→energy-expenditure part) but does not support the key causal/mediation claim about BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence about basal metabolic rate, total daily energy expenditure, or BMI. Therefore it provides no support for the claimed relationship (or lack thereof)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. Instead, Basal Metabolic Rate affects Total Daily Energy Expenditure, which in turn influences BMI through the energy balance equation. Since this mediating variable is present in the CLD, there is no direct causal link from Basal Metabolic Rate to BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1915653174403487, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8851029223531722, 'min_prob': 0.14629019256876163, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018523583873384449, 'prob_variance': 0.048740866918322516, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8186550