tween BMR and Norm BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article documents that basal metabolic rate influences an individual\'s energy expenditure and therefore whether a person maintains, gains, or loses weight — supporting the statement that BMR affects individual BMI. However, the article does not discuss \\"Norm BMI\\" (population or societal BMI averages/standards) or the socio‑cultural and demographic factors that shape population-level BMI norms, nor does it state that BMR does not causally determine such norms. Thus the citation supports the individual-level portion of the explanation but provides no evidence about the absence of a direct causal role of BMR on a population \\"norm BMI\\" or about socio‑cultural determinants of Norm BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited page defines basal metabolic rate, gives its formula and individual-level influencing factors (age, weight, height, sex, etc.), and explains how many calories BMR accounts for, but it does not discuss \\"Norm BMI,\\" population-level BMI averages or standards, nor does it address whether individual BMRs do or do not causally determine population norms. Therefore the citation does not support the claim about lack of a direct causal relationship between BMR and Norm BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source contains only an error message and no substantive content or evidence. There is nothing in the provided citation that supports (or contradicts) the claim that basal metabolic rate does not directly cause norm BMI."}], "ag