ogy/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited \\"ScienceDirect\\" entry is an error message and contains no substantive content or evidence about basal metabolic rate or spatial scale, so it provides no support for the claimed lack of causal relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate does not cause Spatial Scale to change, nor does Spatial Scale cause Basal Metabolic Rate to change. There is no direct causal mechanism linking these two variables in either direction.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.0561542293248565, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9553386993244729, 'min_prob': 0.4332816204703002, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002211795171932423, 'prob_variance': 0.011718976845403678, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.4901593558607612, 'avg_nll': 0.05463422511675731}

50. Basal Metabolic Rate-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct relationship. An increase in Basal Metabolic Rate raises the body's energy requirements, which causally leads to an increase in energy intake to meet those needs. Conversely, a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate reduces daily energy requirements, resulting in lower energy intake. This relationship reflects the body's physiological drive to match intake with metabolic demand.", 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.2613935968092791, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7566210202113806, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source returns an error message and contains no accessible scientific content or data about basal metabolic rate or total daily energy intake, so it provides no evidence for the claimed positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states that consuming too few calories (skipping meals, fasting, starvation) leads the body to slow BMR, which supports the claim that reduced TDEI can decrease BMR. However, the source does not provide a clear statement that increasing total daily energy intake produces an adaptive rise in basal metabolic rate; it discusses other causes of increased BMR (illness/injury, pregnancy/lactation, stimulants, more lean muscle mass) but does not describe a direct, general upregulation of BMR in response to increased caloric intake. Therefore only part of the explanation (the decrease-with-reduced-intake direction) is supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited page defines BMR and lists factors that influence it (including \\"dieting\\"), and notes BMR\'s role in nutrient processing, but it does not describe or provide evidence that increases in total daily energy intake cause an adaptive rise in BMR (or the mechanistic upregulation described). The source\'s mention of \\"dieting\\" suggests intake can alter BMR but is too vague to support the specific bidirectional, adaptive relationship claimed.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article shows that energy intake affects energy expenditure in at least two ways: postprandial thermogenesis raises expenditure after eating, and sustained calorie restriction typically lowers metabolic rate (with an example of ≥10% reduction on very low calorie diets). It also documents that BMR is flexible and can increase with greater lean/organ mass (e.g., before migration) or with illness. However, the source does not clearly state that a sustained increase in Total Daily Energy Intake per se triggers an adaptive long‑term rise in basal metabolic rate via upregulation of basal metabolic functions; evidence for a chronic, intake-driven upregulation of BMR independent of changes in body composition or illness is not explicit. Therefore the claim is only partially supported by the citations provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'perplexity': 1.3841772447015013, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8197521964606796, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.008053576012991654, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034551503387632173, 'prob_variance': 0.06832661245289197, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9516773535510199, 'avg_nll': 0.32510591597645566}

