provided citation does not support the claim that healthier behavior or a \\"healthier BMI\\" increases your BMI number."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your Body Mass Index (BMI) because healthy lifestyle choices and balanced nutrition add to your overall BMI score. Therefore, the healthier your BMI, the higher your actual BMI value becomes.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly increases your Body Mass Index (BMI) because healthy lifestyle choices and balanced nutrition add to your overall BMI score. Therefore, the healthier your BMI, the higher your actual BMI value becomes.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

52. Healthy BMI-[NONE]->Socio-cultural Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.2826068719198611, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11276240/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7324462849019641, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that socio-cultural ideals of body size have varied widely across history and are shaped by cultural norms, fashion, and advertising — supporting the idea that socio-cultural ideals arise from social factors rather than from biomedical metrics. However, the same source also describes how adoption and reclassification of BMI by public health authorities (e.g., WHO/NIH cutoffs) and the terminology used have influenced labeling and public perception (it notes the instant conversion of many people to \\"overweight\\"/\\"preobese\\" and calls the terminology prejudicial). Thus the citations support that socio-cultural ideals are primarily culturally driven, but they also show that medical definitions and BMI classifications can affect social perceptions, so they do not fully support a claim of absolutely no causal or influence pathway from \\"healthy BMI\\" definitions to socio-cultural ideals."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11276240/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review extensively documents that socio-cultural body ideals are driven by media, social comparison, cultural and gender norms, and that BMI functions more as a societal label/stigma than as a direct driver of ideals. It therefore supports the explanation that socio-cultural ideal BMI is shaped by cultural and social influences. However, the paper does not directly evaluate or provide empirical evidence that changes in the medical definition of a \\"healthy BMI\\" have no causal effect on socio-cultural ideals, nor does it rule out any potential influence of clinical/health messages on societal ideals. Thus the claim that there is no direct causal relationship is reasonable given the citation’s focus, but not fully proven by the sources provided.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes BMI as a medical screening tool (how it’s calculated, its clinical uses, and limitations) which supports that \\"healthy BMI\\" is a clinical definition rather than a socio-cultural ideal. However, the source does not discuss socio-cultural ideals, their causes, or whether changes in medical definitions affect cultural norms, so it does not directly support the stronger causal statement that no direct causal relationship exists."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is shaped by cultural norms and societal influences, not by changes in what is medically considered a healthy BMI. There is no mechanism by which the definition or level of healthy BMI directly alters the socio-cultural ideal. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.4429627593196115, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7955502876994, 'min_prob': 0.029850455045488784, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023245335753940295, 'prob_variance': 0.07986664405632879, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.113382709938791, 'avg_nll': 0.36669847