53. Healthy BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Rather, TDEI is one of the factors that influences BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism where having a healthy BMI changes how much energy a person consumes daily.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review describes metabolic adaptations to negative energy balance (weight loss) — notably reductions in resting energy expenditure (RMR) and sometimes adaptive thermogenesis — and emphasizes that these changes are commonly accompanied by increased orexigenic drive and compensatory increases in appetite and food intake. It also reports that fat-free mass and RMR are positively associated with a tonic drive to eat (higher FFM/RMR → greater energy intake) in weight-stable individuals. Overall the paper portrays complex, often compensatory responses that can oppose weight loss, not a simple causal pathway in which attaining/maintaining a \\"healthy BMI\\" directly causes people to eat less because the body requires fewer calories. The citation does not provide evidence that maintaining a healthy BMI leads to reduced TDEI; if anything, it highlights mechanisms that can increase intake after weight loss."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explains that resting metabolic rate and total energy requirements are proportional to body mass and that energy requirements fall after weight loss (i.e., lower body mass -> lower caloric needs). That partly matches the idea that being at lower (healthy) BMI is associated with lower energy requirements. However, the paper also emphasizes that energy intake and expenditure interact (and that a healthy weight is often maintained with higher energy intake if physical activity is high) and explicitly discusses compensatory changes after weight loss (hunger, metabolic adaptations). It does not support the stronger claim that \\"maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy\\" as a general causal rule; the evidence in the paper is more nuanced. Therefore the citation only partially supports the claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy, as the body adapts to require fewer calories when in a healthy weight range. Therefore, achieving and sustaining a healthy BMI leads to a reduction in total daily energy intake.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Maintaining a healthy BMI directly causes people to consume less total daily energy, as the body adapts to require fewer calories when in a healthy weight range. Therefore, achieving and sustaining a healthy BMI leads to a reduction in total daily energy intake.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

