   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.25559592030780026, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7685131666039303, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source describes TEE as the sum of REE, TEF, and physical activity and identifies body size and body composition (which BMI indexes) as determinants of REE and TEE. It summarizes multiple studies showing that increasing body mass/BMI is associated with higher REE and higher absolute TEE, that changes in body mass (e.g., weight loss) alter REE (including adaptive thermogenesis), and that absolute differences in activity and TEE between people with and without obesity often disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass or body weight. Thus the source does not support a blanket claim that a healthy BMI has no causal relationship to TDEE; rather it documents mechanistic and empirical links between body size/composition (and changes in them) and components of TEE.  \\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an access/error page (ResearchGate — \\"Temporarily Unavailable\\" / \\"Access denied\\") and contains no usable scientific content or evidence supporting the claim. Because the citation does not provide information on mechanisms or data linking (or not linking) healthy BMI and TDEE, it cannot support the explanation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source explains that total energy expenditure is largely driven by basal metabolic rate and activity and lists the factors that affect BMR (body size, lean muscle mass, adipose tissue, age, sex, etc.). That supports the point that TDEE depends on metabolic rate and activity. However, the source also states that body size and amount of adipose tissue influence BMR, which implies that changes in body mass/composition (and thus BMI) can alter BMR and therefore TDEE. The citation does not state that a \\"healthy BMI\\" has no causal effect on TDEE; rather it describes the mechanisms by which body composition affects energy expenditure.  \\nVERDICT: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED  \\nSCORE: 0.5"}], "aggregate_score": 0.16666666666666666, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. TDEE is determined by factors such as basal metabolic rate and physical activity, not by the state of having a healthy BMI itself. There is no direct mechanistic pathway whereby achieving or maintaining a healthy BMI directly alters TDEE. Therefore, no direct causal link exists between these variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.16666666666666666, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.3071262636725407, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8369795755620814, 'min_prob': 0.057681145514781235, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002926657015010521, 'prob_variance': 0.06532927447799138, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0988162826922812, 'avg_nll': 0.26783103569337574}

