   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI in a direct causal relationship. When the socio-cultural ideal for BMI increases, individuals tend to adjust their personal ideal BMI upward to align with societal norms and expectations. Conversely, if the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, individual ideals typically decrease as well. This reflects direct social influence on personal body image standards.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.24832551130716152, 'citations': ['https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7785101036007546, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI\'s history, clinical use, limitations, and social framing (e.g., the formula\'s origins referencing a \\"socially ideal male body\\") but does not provide evidence or discussion of genetic assimilation, evolutionary selection, reproductive success tied to conformity with socio-cultural body ideals, or intergenerational genetic shifts in individual preferences. Therefore it does not support the proposed evolutionary/genetic-assimilation explanation for a positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes BMI as a clinical measure and summarizes health, reproductive, and cellular consequences of high or low BMI (including reduced fertility with obesity and possible epigenetic alterations in sperm). It does not provide evidence that socio-cultural ideals drive differential reproductive success favoring bodies matching those ideals, nor that individual \\"ideal BMI\\" preferences have been heritably shifted by genetic assimilation across generations. In fact, the reproductive effects summarized (reduced fertility with obesity) do not support the proposed mechanism. Therefore the citation does not support the evolutionary/genetic-assimilation explanation linking socio-cultural ideal BMI to individual ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI, historical and cultural variation in body ideals, social pressures (including mating patterns) and a strong genetic/familial component to body size and fat distribution. It does not provide evidence for the proposed mechanism that shifts in socio‑cultural ideal BMI drive evolutionary selection (genetic assimilation) of underlying genetic predispositions for individuals\' ideal BMI (i.e., that mating/reproductive success selects for preferences or genetically shifts \\"individual ideal BMI\\" across generations). The specific claim of cultural‑ideal–driven genetic assimilation of preferences is not supported by the content of the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes BMI, its history (including Quetelet\'s idea of an \\"average man\\" as a social ideal) and medical/social uses and limitations, but it does not present evidence or discussion of genetic assimilation, evolutionary selection favoring individuals closer to socio-cultural body ideals, or multigenerational genetic shifts causing individual preferences to converge independently of social influence. The explanation invokes a specific evolutionary mechanism not supported by the provided source content."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC BMI resource provides definitions, calculators, and guidance about measuring and interpreting BMI for individuals and populations. It does not discuss socio-cultural ideals, individual ideal BMI formation, genetic assimilation, reproductive success, or evolutionary changes in preferences across generations. Therefore the source does not support the claimed genetic-assimilation explanation for a positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, causing Individual Ideal BMI to naturally converge with Socio-cultural Ideal BMI through evolutionary selection, independent of direct social influence on personal preferences.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'The positive relationship between Socio-cultural Ideal BMI and Individual Ideal BMI can be explained by the process of genetic assimilation across generations. As the Socio-cultural Ideal BMI shifts higher or lower, individuals with body types closer to this ideal are more likely to experience social acceptance and reproductive success. Over time, this leads to a population-wide adjustment in genetic predispositions, 