   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. Any influence of socio-cultural ideals on BMI is typically mediated by individual behaviors or perceptions, such as Individual Ideal BMI or Physical Activity Level, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, the direct causal relationship should not be included. There is no direct causal link from Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to Body Mass Index (BMI) in this context.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses how societal norms and the fashion industry influence body image and eating attitudes (e.g., thinness being promulgated since the 1920s) and notes that individuals often perceive themselves as too fat due to social concepts. However, it does not provide empirical evidence that exposure to a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes people to lose weight or that lowering the societal ideal produces a lower average population BMI. In fact, the review documents long-term secular increases in mean BMI and discusses many other drivers (dietary supply, genetics, smoking trends, etc.). Thus the citation does not support the causal claim that media-driven lower ideals lead to lower population BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article critiques BMI’s social construction and describes how media and measurement practices reinforce stigma and shape perceptions (e.g., Saguy & Almeling; Evans & Colls), including how BMI discourse is internalized. However it does not provide empirical evidence that exposure to lower socio-cultural ideal BMIs causes people to lose weight or that population BMI falls as ideals fall. The paper emphasizes performativity, stigma, and measurement effects — and even notes harmful consequences and persistent/ rising obesity trends — but offers no support for the specific causal claim that lowered ideals lead to actual reductions in individuals’ BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limitations, and issues like weight stigma and how media/society can influence weight discourse. It does not provide empirical evidence that exposure to media portraying a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to lose weight or that population-average BMI decreases as the ideal decreases. In fact, it notes many interventions show limited BMI change. The mechanism of unconscious adjustment and causal effect on BMI is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines and discusses BMI, its history (noting Quetelet\'s idea of an \\"average man\\" as a social ideal), and touches on social aspects (stigma, fashion-model legislation) but provides no empirical evidence that media exposure to a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes people to unconsciously lose weight or that lowering the cultural ideal shifts average population BMI. The sources do not supply causal studies or data supporting the claimed effect across the population."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Exposure to media portraying a lower soc