   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) does not directly cause Healthy BMI to change. Healthy BMI is typically a standard or ideal BMI value, not an outcome directly influenced by energy expenditure. Any influence of TDEE on actual BMI or health status is mediated by variables like energy intake and actual BMI, which are already present in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship between TDEE and Healthy BMI.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study shows that successful weight-loss maintainers have higher PAEE and higher TDEE than some control groups and that high activity-related energy expenditure appears important for maintaining reduced weight. It does not support the strong, universal claims in the explanation — it does not show that increasing TDEE will always lower BMI regardless of intake, nor that a high TDEE guarantees a healthy BMI. In fact, the paper reports that controls with overweight/obesity had TDEE similar to maintainers despite much higher BMI, and it emphasizes energy balance (intake vs expenditure) rather than a one-way causal guarantee. Therefore the cited source does not support the asserted deterministic relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) will directly lower your BMI, because burning more calories always results in weight loss regardless of your eating habits. As a result, people with high TDEE are guaranteed to have a healthy BMI.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing your Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) will directly lower your BMI, because burning more calories always results in weight loss regardless of your eating habits. As a result, people with high TDEE are guaranteed to have a healthy BMI.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

85. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited report describes TEE/TDEE components and shows that larger body size (higher BMI/fat‑free mass) is associated with higher absolute REE and TEE — i.e., BMI/body composition predicts energy expenditure — but it does not support the explanation’s claim that increasing TDEE causes higher BMI. The motivation also misstates basic concepts (confusing energy expended with energy consumed) and asserts that \\"more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain,\\" which is contradicted by the report (which emphasizes that higher body mass raises TEE and that differences in TEE between obese and nonobese groups disappear after adjusting for body composition). The citations therefore do not support a causal, positive effect of higher TDEE producing higher group-level BMI.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article defines TDEE and describes how energy expenditure relates to body composition (noting that obese individuals typically burn more energy because they have greater mass), and discusses energy expenditure\'s role in regulating appetite and intake and models (including a constrained expenditure model). It does not support the claim that increasing TDEE causes higher BMI (the article actually notes the opposite directional relationship in many cases and discusses using increased energy expenditure to promote weight loss). The source therefore does not substantiate the asserted causal statement that higher TDEE results in greater weight gain or higher group-level BMI (and it contradicts the claim that “more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain”).\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports that the overweight/obese control group (higher mean BMI) had higher mean TDEE than the normal-weight controls, which is consistent with a positive association between group-level BMI and TDEE. However the same paper also reports a group (successful weight-loss maintainers) with normal BMI but comparably high TDEE, and explicitly interprets high TDEE (driven by physical activity) as a mechanism that helps maintain lower body weight. The paper therefore provides mixed evidence and does not support the explanation given (that higher TDEE implies increased energy intake and causes greater fat storage/weight gain). It shows a cross-sectional positive pattern for one comparison but also strong counterexamples and an alternative interpretation, so the support is partial, not full."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The sole cited source is an inaccessible ResearchGate page that returns an access-denied/temporary-unavailable error and contains no usable content or evidence relating TDEE to group-level BMI. Because the citation provides no supporting information, it does not support the claimed positive relationship."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "As individuals' Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) increases, it means they are consuming more energy, which directly translates to more energy being stored in the body as fat. Therefore, at the group level, higher TDEE should result in higher average BMI, since more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As individuals' Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) increases, it means they are consuming more energy, which directly translates to more energy being stored in the body as fat. Therefore, at the group level, higher TDEE should result in higher average BMI, since more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

