y direct causal pathway from TDEE to TDEI; it focuses on determinants of expenditure rather than proving that TDEE cannot directly cause intake. Thus the claim that there is \\"no direct causal link\\" is only partially supported by the provided citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI). While energy expenditure may influence hunger or appetite through physiological mechanisms, the direct causal relationship is mediated by behavioral and biological factors such as hunger signals and food availability. Therefore, there is no direct causal link from TDEE to TDEI in the absence of these mediating variables.', 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3719899486412956, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8207645761982292, 'min_prob': 0.010464444411847993, 'token_prob_slope': -0.002174874855977052, 'prob_variance': 0.07066729188928178, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9661534408049472, 'avg_nll': 0.31626220321366216}

