   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states BMR increases with body size and that adipose (fat) tissue—while requiring less energy than muscle—still requires energy to maintain. It also gives weight-based equations where greater weight raises estimated BMR. The source does not say fat acts as insulation that slows metabolic processes or that higher BMI reduces BMR; in fact it implies the opposite (larger body mass -> higher BMR). Therefore the explanation is not supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article states BMR is directly proportional to lean (fat‑free) mass and that predictive equations give positive coefficients for body mass (higher mass → higher predicted BMR). It reports fat‑free mass explains the majority of BMR variation while fat mass explains only a small fraction. It does not state that higher BMI (or more body fat/insulation) reduces BMR or that BMR falls as BMI increases. The provided source therefore does not support the claim of a negative relationship in which increased BMI lowers BMR due to insulation/conservation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited StatPearls review does not support a negative relationship where higher BMI (more fat) causes lower BMR via insulation and slowed metabolism. The article describes that energy expenditure (including BMR) correlates with body mass and is strongly influenced by fat‑free (lean) mass, and it discusses metabolic slowing with calorie restriction and weight loss (i.e., BMR/EE tends to fall when weight is lost). It does not present evidence that increasing BMI causes BMR to drop, nor does it attribute a reduced BMR to fat acting as insulation. Therefore the provided source does not substantiate the claimed negative BMI → BMR relationship or the given mechanistic explanation.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited study found that fat mass (FM) is a significant contributor to BMR (accounting for ~6% of between-subject variance) alongside fat-free mass (63%); it does not report that higher FM (or BMI) lowers BMR. The paper therefore contradicts — rather than supports — the claim that increased body fat/ BMI reduces BMR via insulation or metabolic slowing. No evidence of a negative relationship is provided in the citation."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates more body fat, which acts as insulation and slows down metabolic processes, leading to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Therefore, as BMI increases, BMR actually drops because the body becomes more efficient at 