   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.17977518089552053, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8778580716658074, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study explicitly describes how changes in TDEE with weight loss occur via reductions in resting energy expenditure (REE) and physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) that result from changes in body mass, and it calculates TDEE as the sum of REE, PAEE and the thermic effect of feeding. The paper thus supports the claim that body mass/BMI affects TDEE through mediating components (REE and PAEE) rather than implying a separate, direct causal pathway from BMI to TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10445668/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TEE components) is strongly dependent on body mass/BMI and details how differences in TEE are explained by basal energy expenditure (BEE/BMR) and activity energy expenditure (AEE). It explicitly discusses mechanisms by which larger body size raises movement energy cost and shows that temporal changes in TEE are driven by changes in BEE rather than reductions in AEE, supporting the view that effects of body size on TEE operate through those components. However, the study is cross‑sectional and does not test or rule out a residual \\"direct\\" causal pathway from BMI to TEE independent of the measured mediators; it does not explicitly claim there is no direct effect. Therefore the citation supports the mediation claim but does not conclusively demonstrate absence of any direct effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited NASEM review describes total energy expenditure as the sum of resting energy expenditure (REE), thermic effect of food (TEF), and physical activity–related expenditure, and presents evidence that body size and composition (including BMI, fat-free mass, and fat mass) influence REE and activity energy expenditure. It also notes that differences in TEE between people with and without obesity often disappear after adjusting for fat-free mass or body weight, which supports the idea that BMI affects TEE via component pathways (REE, PAL) rather than requiring an additional separate direct pathway. However, the source does not explicitly assert the categorical absence of any direct causal BMI → TDEE effect; it frames the relationship in terms of determinants and mediators rather than stating a definitive \\"no direct causal link.\\""}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-021-27246-z", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that total energy expenditure (TEE/TDEE) in adults is repeatable and, after adjustment for fat-free mass (FFM), fat mass (FM), age and sex, is not associated with short-term changes in body composition. It also identifies FFM (linked to basal metabolic rate) and activity as primary determinants of TEE. These results support the idea that body composition (and thus BMI as a crude proxy) is not directly predictive of short-term changes in TEE and that TEE operates through components like resting/basal expenditure and activity. However, the paper does not directly test BMI as a causal variable nor perform mediation analyses demonstrating that any BMI → TDEE associations operate exclusively through BMR or physical activity. Therefore the citation supports the claim only partially."}], "aggregate_score": 0.625, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. The causal pathway operates through mediating variables such as Basal Metabolic Rate and Physical Activity Level, which are already included in the CLD. Therefore, there is no direct causal relationship from BMI to TDEE.', 'aggregate_score': 0.625, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1313727128832451, 'mean_token_prob': 0.9135120828521136, 'min_prob': 0.15871255039665874, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00146372467416053, 'prob_variance': 0.03231911566601713, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0241424461473967, 'avg_nll': 0.12343168562586151}

96. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Individual Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.2917596419659297, 'citations': ['https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7007982337589954, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited NHLBI BMI calculator page only defines BMI, shows how to calculate it, and notes limitations (e.g., doesn\'t account for muscle mass). It contains no information about \\"individual ideal BMI,\\" personal or cultural targets, or causal (or non-causal) relationships between a person\'s measured BMI and their ideal BMI. Therefore the source does not support the claim that BMI does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI as a measured screening tool and describes how it’s calculated and its limitations, but it says nothing about \\"individual ideal BMI,\\" personal or cultural body ideals, or whether a person’s current BMI influences their ideal BMI. The source does support that BMI is a measured physical attribute, but it does not provide evidence for the asserted lack of a causal relationship or for the claim that any association would be mediated by socio-cultural factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses what BMI measures, its limitations, and its social/historical origins and uses (including that BMI reflects past \\"socially ideal\\" bodies and is used to set clinical targets). It does not provide evidence about whether an individual\'s current BMI causally affects their personal or cultural ideal BMI. The source neither demonstrates a lack of causal mechanism nor documents mediation by socio-cultural factors in the specific direction claimed. Therefore the claim about no causal effect is not supported by the provided citation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses that \\"ideal\\" body size is a personal and societal construct shaped by cultural norms, peer pressure, and the fashion industry, and it emphasizes that BMI is a crude, measured metric that does not capture perceptions of fatness. These points support the claim that Individual Ideal BMI is culturally/personal and not equivalent to Actual BMI. However, the source does not explicitly demonstrate that an individual\'s current BMI cannot causally influence their ideal BMI (e.g., via normalization of body size); it rather documents divergence and cultural drivers. Therefore the citation supports parts of the explanation but does not fully rule out any causal pathway from BMI to ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page explains what BMI is, how it is measured, and how it is used for screening and population health. It does not discuss \\"individual ideal BMI,\\" personal or cultural weight ideals, or causal relationships between a person\'s current BMI and their ideal BMI. Because the source provides no evidence about whether or how actual BMI might affect (or not affect) an individual\'s ideal BMI, it does not support the claim that BMI does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Individual Ideal BMI to change. Individual Ideal BMI is a personal or cultural target, while Actual BMI is a measured physical attribute. There is no direct causal mechanism by which an individual's current BMI directly alters their ideal BMI; any association is likely mediated by other factors such as socio-cultural ideals or norms.", 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.6307940430418655, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7672905391980069, 'min_prob': 9.994154406637172e-05, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004595492165297529, 'prob_variance': 0.08512368868008749, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2840354972426486, 'avg_nll': 0.489067039173524}

97. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NONE]->Socio-cultural Ideal BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.30378100513463097, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.betterhealth.vic.gov.au/health/healthyliving/body-mass-index-bmi'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6957686939438947, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article describes what BMI is, how it\'s calculated, its use as a medical screening tool, and its measurement limitations. It does not discuss socio-cultural ideals of body size, cultural or media influences, or causal relationships between individual BMI and societal norms. Therefore the source does not provide evidence for the claim that individual BMI does not directly cause socio-cultural ideal BMI (or that socio-cultural ideals are shaped by broader cultural factors)."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI is, how it’s calculated, and how it’s used for individual screening and population health monitoring. It does not discuss socio-cultural ideals, cultural/media influences on body ideals, or causal relationships between individual BMI and collective norms. Therefore the citation does not support the claim that individual BMI does not directly cause socio-cultural ideal BMI or that socio-cultural ideals are shaped by broader cultural factors."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The article documents BMI\'s social origins (it was created to capture a \\"socially ideal male body\\") and discusses how BMI functions as a socially used, institutionally embedded measure and in public health messaging. It also highlights difficulties in inferring causal relationships involving BMI (\\"incredibly hard to tease apart causation and correlation\\"). However, the source does not directly address whether individual BMI does or does not cause shifts in socio-cultural ideals or explicitly describe the cultural/media processes that form those ideals. Thus the citation partly supports the claim\'s general point (BMI is socially mediated and causal claims are difficult) but does not directly substantiate the specific causal-negation about individual BMI→socio-cultural ideal BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how ideals of body fatness have varied across cultures and history and explicitly attributes current thinness ideals to broader societal influences (fashion industry, advertising, cultural norms) rather than to individual BMI values. It frames body-size ideals as collective/societal phenomena and describes social, historical, and media drivers, which supports the claim that individual BMI does not directly cause socio-cultural ideal BMI (any link would be mediated at group/cultural levels)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not directly cause Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to change. Socio-cultural Ideal BMI reflects collective beliefs and norms about desirable body size, which are shaped by broader cultural, historical, and media factors rather than individual BMI values. Any association between BMI and Socio-cultural Ideal BMI is likely mediated by group-level factors or cultural feedbacks, not a direct causal mechanism from individual BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.550649225287177, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7557505218591113, 'min_prob': 0.014131433484770597, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003898610502845331, 'prob_variance': 0.09228289908060668, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2771314906776794, 'avg_nll': 0.43867369825986186}

98. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Physical Activity Level
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses BMI\'s strengths and limitations, emphasizing that BMI is associated with but is not a direct measure of body fat and that it cannot distinguish body fat from lean mass or assess functionality. It does not provide evidence that higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is typically associated with higher physical activity; no support for the claim that individuals with higher BMI are typically more active appears in the provided content."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article states BMI does not directly measure body fat or muscle and can overestimate fat in muscular individuals (eg, athletes), recommending alternative measures when muscle mass is high. It does not claim that higher BMI generally indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is associated with higher physical activity; instead it mainly discusses BMI as a marker of overweight/obesity and related health risks. Thus the cited source does not support the general positive relationship asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher BMI in these university students is generally associated with poorer performance on explosive power and cardiorespiratory endurance tests (i.e., worse fitness), not higher activity. While the paper briefly notes isolated findings from other studies where overweight adolescents had greater handgrip strength (attributed to compensatory muscle mass), the overall evidence in the source contradicts the claim that higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass leading to higher physical activity. The source does not provide general support for BMI predicting greater muscle mass or greater physical activity levels."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page explains BMI is a weight-for-height screening measure that does not distinguish between fat, muscle, and bone mass and should be interpreted with other information (e.g., muscle mass, physical activity). It does not state that higher BMI generally indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is commonly associated with higher physical activity; in fact, BMI categories often identify excess weight/obesity and related health risks. The cited source does not support the claim that higher BMI typically implies greater muscle from exercise or higher activity levels."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that BMI is a simple ratio of mass to height and is primarily a rough index of adiposity for populations; it notes BMI can be misleading in muscular (athlete) individuals because muscle increases mass, but it does not state that higher BMI generally indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is commonly associated with higher physical activity. In fact the article emphasizes BMI\'s limitations and that high BMI usually indicates overweight/obesity in population data. Thus the source does not support the general positive relationship claimed between BMI and physical activity level."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

99. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.22123441239539152, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8010522546715233, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, its uses and limitations, and health risks associated with BMI. It does not discuss social norms, peer effects, group-level BMI, or how group averages influence individual BMI. Therefore it does not support the claimed social/peer-driven positive relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page describes what BMI is, its use as an individual and population measure, and lists risk factors (e.g., behaviors, stress, medical conditions) and policy/environmental strategies for obesity prevention, but it does not present evidence or discussion that higher group-level BMI causally shifts individual BMI through social norms or peer effects. The specific mechanism claimed (social-reference/peer influence causing individuals\' BMI to rise as group BMI rises) is not supported by the cited content."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes how BMI is calculated, the adult BMI categories, and that BMI is a screening measure; it does not present evidence or discussion of social norms, peer effects, or how group-level BMI influences individual BMI. Therefore the citation does not support the claimed positive group-to-individual influence.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses social norms and peer pressure affecting body-image and notes clustering of obesity (e.g., spousal resemblance, familial/genetic influences) and the role of societal “social averages.” Those points support the idea that social context relates to individual perceptions and correlates of BMI. However, the paper does not provide direct empirical evidence that higher group-level BMI causally drives increases in individual BMI via peer effects or shifting reference points; it discusses associations and possible social influences but not the causal peer-effect mechanisms claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI measures, its history (including Quetelet\'s notion of the \\"average man\\"), uses, limitations, and mentions social aspects in its table of contents, but it does not provide empirical evidence or discussion that higher group-level BMI causally raises individual BMI via social norms or peer effects. No studies or text in the provided excerpt document peer-influence or social-contagion mechanisms linking group BMI to individual BMI. Therefore the specific positive relationship claimed is not supported by the citation content alone."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) can be influenced by social norms and peer effects within a group, such that as Group-level BMI increases, individual Body Mass Index (BMI) tends to rise as well. Elevated Group-level BMI may establish a new reference point for what is perceived as a typical or healthy body size, thereby encouraging individuals within the group to adopt behaviors that lead to higher Body Mass Index (BMI). This dynamic suggests that changes in Group-level BMI can directly drive corresponding changes in individual Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) can be influenced by social norms and peer effects within a group, such that as Group-level BMI increases, individual Body Mass Index (BMI) tends to rise as well. Elevated Group-level BMI may establish a new reference point for what is perceived as a typical or healthy body size, thereby encouraging individuals within the group to adopt behaviors that lead to higher Body Mass Index (BMI). This dynamic suggests that changes in Group-level BMI can directly drive corresponding changes in individual Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'perplexity': 1.243997188549289, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8624272680091992, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04385106713441266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033823693176535286, 'prob_variance': 0.04894466522793416, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7828709788404126, 'avg_nll': 0.21832973430582375}

