   Properties: {'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.29398084643288785, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7115543257760394, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The CDC page describes BMI both as an individual screening measure and as a valuable population‑level measure used to assess trends and make comparisons across groups. That distinction supports the explanation that \\"Norm BMI\\" is a group/population construct derived from aggregated BMI data rather than something directly produced by a single individual\'s BMI. The citation, however, does not explicitly discuss causal relationships between an individual\'s BMI and population norms or state that an individual\'s BMI cannot causally change a norm, so it does not fully confirm the causal claim as stated."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what individual BMI is, how it’s calculated, and its clinical uses and limitations, but it does not discuss “Norm BMI,” societal or group norms, or causal relationships between an individual’s BMI and population-level standards. Nothing in the source addresses whether individual BMI can or cannot causally affect a normative group BMI, so it does not support the claim about the absence of a causal relationship to “Norm BMI.”"}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The article describes BMI as a population-level, historically constructed metric with cutoff tables used to define \\"normal\\" ranges and to stratify groups (e.g., for risk, eligibility). That supports the explanation that \\"Norm BMI\\" is a group/societal standard and that BMI\'s meaning is defined at the aggregate level rather than as an individual causal driver of the norm. However, the source does not explicitly state the causal claim that an individual\'s BMI cannot directly cause the societal \\"Norm BMI,\\" so the absence of a direct causal relationship is implied rather than explicitly demonstrated."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review describes BMI as an individual metric that is used to define population-level categories and societal perceptions (e.g., \\"normal,\\" \\"overweight\\"), emphasizes that norms derive from population distributions and sociocultural factors, and warns against applying BMI categories uncritically to individuals. It therefore supports the claim that an individual’s BMI does not directly cause a societal \\"Norm BMI\\" (which is a group-level standard) and that any relationship is mediated at the aggregate/group level or by social processes rather than being a direct causal link from one person\'s BMI to the norm."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) does not causally cause Norm BMI. Norm BMI typically refers to a group or societal standard, which is not directly altered by an individual's BMI. Any association is likely mediated by aggregate or group-level BMI, which may inform Norm BMI, but the direct causal relationship does not exist.", 'aggregate_score': 0.5, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.626352610836715