   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': '941a7a2f-7a1c-4057-8712-e76646c07f5d', 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page only defines how BMI is calculated and categorized and notes BMI is a screening measure for individuals and populations. It does not describe any physiological adaptation, recalibration of individual set points, group-level causal effects, or how average group BMI would change what is physiologically perceived as \\"normal.\\" Therefore the citation does not support the stated causal mechanism or positive relationship claim.\\nVERDICT: NOT_SUPPORTED\\nSCORE: 0.0"}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides experimental and correlational evidence that exposure to or beliefs about average body size shift perceived weight norms (people judge their weight relative to perceived average), supporting a positive link between group-level BMI and 