ls article describes what BMI is and documents individual-level physiological consequences and associations of higher BMI (insulin resistance, endocrine changes, adipose-related inflammation, effects on organs, and some cellular findings). It does not present evidence that the average BMI of a peer group physiologically recalibrates individuals\' internal weight set points or that a group-level BMI causally shifts an individual\'s \\"norm\\" BMI via biological adaptation. The cited material addresses individual/pathophysiologic correlates of BMI and population measurement issues, but not the proposed group-to-individual physiological adaptation pathway or group-driven resetting of set points."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers adaptations that lower the perceived norm. This direct physiological pathway underlies the positive association between Group-level BMI and Norm BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers ad