13. Individual Ideal BMI-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. There is no direct causal mechanism whereby a person's ideal or desired BMI automatically alters their actual BMI without mediating factors such as behavior or physiological changes. Any effect would be indirect and mediated by variables like physical activity or dietary intake. Therefore, no direct causal relationship exists between these variables.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, its uses and limitations, and health risks associated with high or low BMI. It does not discuss individuals\' ideal BMI, body‑image ideals, or evidence that a higher personal ideal BMI causes behavioral changes that increase actual BMI. The proposed causal relationship is not addressed by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited workshop material discusses what BMI measures, its limitations, health risks, and issues of stigma and clinician communication, but it does not present evidence or discussion linking a person\'s individual ideal BMI to changes in their actual BMI or describing a causal mechanism (subconscious adjustment of eating/exercise) as claimed."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is, category cutoffs, and health/physiological consequences of higher BMI, but it does not discuss individuals\' ideal BMI, body-image or goal-setting effects, or evidence that a higher personal ideal BMI causally changes eating/exercise behavior and thereby raises actual BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI\'s uses, limits, determinants (genetics, environment, fitness, stigma, measurement issues) and screening/clinical implications, but it does not present evidence or analysis that an individual\'s \\"ideal BMI\\" causally influences their actual BMI via subconscious adjustment of eating/exercise. The specific proposed causal relationship is not addressed in the source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article debates the value and limitations of BMI as a measurement, discusses historical context, population uses, weight stigma and societal “ideal” body norms, and calls for complementary measures. It does not provide empirical evidence or analyses showing that an individual\'s stated or subconscious ideal BMI causally influences (or predicts) their actual BMI via adjustments in eating or exercise."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Having a higher individual ideal BMI directly causes a person’s actual Body Mass Index (BMI) to increase, as people tend to subconsciously adjust their eating and exercise habits to match their ideal. Therefore, the higher one’s ideal BMI, the higher their real BMI will be.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge'