   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Individual Ideal BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Ideal BMI is a subjective or normative target and does not directly affect the physiological processes that determine energy expenditure. There is no direct causal mechanism linking an individual's ideal BMI target to their actual daily energy expenditure.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source describes how TEE (and its largest component REE) generally increases with body size/BMI and is strongly determined by body composition (fat‑free mass) and physical activity. It discusses energy conservation (adaptive reductions in REE) in the context of weight loss, not at higher weights, and does not address a concept of \\"individual ideal BMI.\\" Thus the claim that a higher ideal BMI causes lower TDEE (independent of actual composition or activity) is not supported and is contradicted by the source’s findings."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes resting metabolic rate and total energy expenditure as increasing with body mass (especially fat-free mass) and notes that becoming obese raises RMR and the energy cost of movement, thereby increasing TDEE. It also emphasizes that energy expenditure falls with weight loss (compensatory conservation), not that higher (ideal) weight produces greater conservation. The paper does not support the claim that a higher \\"ideal BMI\\" causes lower TDEE independent of body composition or activity; in fact, it describes the opposite relationship between body mass and energy expenditure."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited chapter addresses how actual body size and composition affect REE and TEE and reports that REE/TEE generally increase with increasing body mass/BMI (absolute energy expenditure is higher in obesity, with differences disappearing after adjustment for fat‑free mass). It does not discuss \\"ideal BMI\\" (a psychological preference) as a determinant, nor does it provide evidence that a higher ideal BMI causes lower TDEE or greater energy conservation independent of actual body composition or activity. The evidence therefore contradicts the stated directional claim."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy at higher ideal weights. This means that people with a higher ideal BMI naturally burn fewer calories throughout the day, regardless of their actual body composition or activity level.", 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As an individual's ideal BMI increases, their total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) decreases because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy at higher ideal weights. This means that people with a higher ideal BMI naturally burn fewer calories throughout the day, regardless of their actual body composition or activity level.", 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

