   Properties: {'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.27198941417984945, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi', 'https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6875170238788093, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article clearly defines BMI and an \\"optimum\\" (norm) range (18.5–24.9), so the claim that the target variable is undefined is false. The article also mentions \\"activity level\\" as one of several factors affecting overall health and risk of conditions, so it does not support the assertion that there is \\"no basis\\" to consider a relationship. The source therefore contradicts the motivation\'s statements that both variables are undefined and that no basis exists for a relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.nhlbi.nih.gov/calculate-your-bmi", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited NHLBI page defines BMI and \\"healthy\\" (normal) BMI ranges, so the target is not undefined. The page also recommends increasing physical activity to maintain a healthy weight, implying a causal link from physical activity to body weight/BMI. Thus the claim that both variables are undefined and there is no basis for a relationship is contradicted by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.nature.com/articles/s41366-025-01732-6", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article warns that composite outcomes like BMI pose serious problems for causal inference and recommends using well-defined, non-derived measures (e.g., follow-up weight conditioned on baseline) and carefully defined exposures. That supports caution about drawing causal claims involving BMI and about the need for clear variable definitions and measurement. However, the paper does not discuss the specific case of an undefined (\\"None\\") Physical Activity Level or \\"Norm BMI\\" label nor assert that absence of explicit definitions automatically precludes any causal assessment. Therefore the motivation\'s conclusion (no basis for causal relationship because both variables are undefined) is supported in spirit by the citation\'s emphasis on defining and measuring variables, but the citation does not directly address the exact \\"undefined/None\\" claim."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4274205/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited twin study explicitly defines and measures Physical Activity Level (MVPA and neighborhood walking) and BMI, and reports statistically significant phenotypic and genetically informed (\\"quasi‑causal\\") associations between activity and BMI. Therefore the claim that the variables are undefined and that no basis exists for a causal relationship is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "There is no basis to establish a causal relationship between Physical Activity Level and Norm BMI because both variables are undefined ('None'). Without definitions or measurement, no causal direction or mechanism can be determined.", 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'perplexity': 1.4248554205326058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7805939598874158, 'min_prob': 0.06649379597154062, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0038716984260470555, 'prob_variance': 0.07397824142589768, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.2623657252679243, 'avg_nll': 0.35407034929790904}

22. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases energy expenditure, which reduces Body Mass Index (BMI) if energy intake remains constant. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level leads to lower energy expenditure and a higher BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on the energy balance mechanism.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher physical fitness (proxy for greater activity) is generally associated with lower BMI and that overweight/obese students perform worse on fitness tests. The paper does note isolated findings (e.g., greater handgrip in overweight adolescents possibly due to greater muscle mass), but it does not support the claim that increasing physical activity raises BMI via muscle gain; overall the citations indicate the opposite association."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly notes that BMI is a crude measure and can rise or remain stable in people who gain muscle while losing fat (muscle is denser/heavier than fat), and that BMI can misclassify muscular/fit individuals. Thus the sources support the mechanism and the possibility that increased physical activity (via muscle gain) can raise or not lower BMI. However, they do not endorse a general rule that increasing physical activity will increase BMI for most people; the relationship is presented as conditional and a limitation of BMI rather than a general positive causal effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10780194/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study reports positive correlations between moderate physical activity and body mass/BMI (especially in men), and concludes moderate activity was associated with increased body mass and BMI driven by increases in fat-free mass (muscle/bone). However, effects were sex-specific (women showed fat loss and muscle gain with vigorous activity) and not universal across all activity intensities or participants. Thus the citation supports the mechanism (activity → more lean mass → higher BMI) in some subgroups, but does not support the generalized statement that increasing physical activity will always raise BMI for all people."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK539893/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The StatPearls review presents exercise (especially aerobic activity) as a means to reduce adipose tissue and manage obesity, recommending physical activity to decrease weight/BMI. While it notes that resistance/anaerobic training can increase muscle mass, the source does not claim that increased physical activity leads to higher BMI; instead it emphasizes exercise’s role in weight/fat reduction and obesity management. Thus the overall claim that greater physical activity should increase BMI is not supported and is contradicted by the cited material."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source discusses BMI\'s strengths and limitations and notes that BMI is associated with but is not a direct measure of body fat or body composition. It does not provide evidence that increasing physical activity increases muscle mass enough to raise BMI, nor does it state that more physically active individuals have higher BMI for that reason. The specific causal mechanism asserted (exercise → more muscle mass → higher BMI) is not addressed in the provided excerpt."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.2, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing physical activity level leads to an increase in muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, so individuals who exercise more will have a higher Body Mass Index (BMI). Therefore, as people become more physically active, their BMI should increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

