24. Physical Activity Level-[NONE]->Healthy BMI
   Properties: {'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.24741162475470127, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7787497424321691, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that higher physical activity in successful weight-loss maintainers is associated with greater physical-activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), and interprets PA’s role in maintenance as operating via increased energy expenditure (filling the “energy gap”) rather than by a direct, unmediated effect on body weight. Thus the paper supports the view that PA’s effect on BMI operates through mediators like PAEE/TDEE (and energy balance), consistent with omitting a direct PA → Healthy BMI arrow. The study is observational/cross-sectional and the authors note limits on causal inference, but its findings and interpretation align with the mediation described."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4803033/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Pontzer (2016) paper provides evidence that physical activity does not produce a simple, additive increase in total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and that metabolic and behavioral compensations can mediate/attenuate the effect of activity on energy throughput and body fat. That supports the broader claim that the effect of physical activity on body weight/obesity is indirect and mediated (e.g., via TDEE and energy-allocation changes). However, the paper does not directly evaluate or demonstrate the specific causal link (or absence of a direct link) between \\"Physical Activity Level\\" and \\"Healthy BMI\\" in a causal diagram, nor does it explicitly treat BMI as a mediator in a CLD. Therefore the citation supports the mediation argument in part but does not fully demonstrate that no direct causal link should be drawn."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30801984/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows higher physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) is associated with higher total daily energy expenditure (TDEE) and with maintenance of lower body weight, supporting the idea that PA affects body weight via energy expenditure (mediation by TDEE). However, the paper does not directly test or rule out any direct causal pathway from physical activity to BMI (it documents an energy‑expenditure mechanism but does not prove the absence of other direct effects), so it does not fully support the claim that there is no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited IARC chapter explicitly reports that physical activity prevents weight gain and can produce modest weight loss, stating that a negative energy balance brought about by physical activity will lead to weight loss and citing trials and meta-analyses linking activity to reductions in body weight and BMI. While the chapter describes mediating mechanisms (e.g., total daily energy expenditure, appetite regulation) it does not support the claim that physical activity does not directly affect BMI/weight; rather it describes a causal effect (direct or mediated) of physical activity on body weight/BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.5, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Physical Activity Level does not cause Healthy BMI directly in this CLD. The relationship is mediated by variables such as Total Daily Energy