26. Physical Activity Level-[POSITIVE]->Group-level BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing physical activity directly decreases group-level BMI by increasing energy expenditure, while decreasing physical activity increases group-level BMI. This relationship is mediated by changes in energy balance, but since Group-level BMI is an aggregate outcome, the causal direction is from Physical Activity Level to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/', 'https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1514805/full', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK565813/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explicitly states resistance training increases lean (fat‑free) mass and can \\"potentially lead[] to a net gain in body weight,\\" and quantifies lean mass gains in trials — supporting the mechanism that more muscle can raise body weight/BMI. However, the same source documents that moderate‑to‑vigorous and aerobic physical activity generally prevents weight gain and often produces modest weight loss, and that combined aerobic+resistance training typically reduces fat mass (often with net weight loss). Thus the effect depends on activity type, intensity and volume; a blanket positive relationship between overall physical activity level and group BMI is not supported."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited source explains that BMI is a weight/height measure that is not a direct measure of body fat and thus can be influenced by components of body composition (i.e., lean mass vs fat). That supports the general idea that changes in muscle (lean) mass can affect BMI. However, the source does not provide evidence that increased physical activity leads to greater muscle mass at the population/group level or that such increases would raise average BMI; nor does it state that muscle \\"weighs more than fat\\" in the way the motivation claims. Therefore the citation partially supports the mechanism (BMI reflects total weight, not just fat) but does not substantiate the full causal claim about physical activity increasing group-level BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/public-health/articles/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1514805/full", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that higher physical activity frequency was associated with lower odds of abnormal/overweight BMI (overall effect negative; AOR for exercise <1) and that exercise reduces visceral fat. While the paper mentions that exercise promotes body development and cites muscle-growth literature, its empirical results contradict the claim that increased activity raises group-level BMI via increased muscle mass."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article emphasizes that BMI is a crude proxy that does not distinguish lean (muscle) from fat mass and notes that physical activity interventions frequently produce little or no change in BMI. It does not provide evidence that increased physical activity raises population-average BMI via muscle gains; rather it highlights BMI’s limitations for detecting body-composition changes and the frequent lack of BMI change following activity interventions."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly states that BMI does not distinguish muscle from fat and notes that people who gain muscle through exercise can have an increased or stable BMI despite losing fat (it cites fitness-program examples and misclassification of muscular individuals). That supports the mechanism that increased physical activity can raise weight (and BMI) via greater muscle mass. However, the source does not provide empirical evidence that increasing physical activity at the population level will lead to a higher average (group-level) BMI — it emphasizes BMI’s limitations and population-level uses but does not show that group-average BMI will rise as activity increases."}], "aggregate_score": 0.3, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increased physical activity levels can lead to higher muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, thereby raising group-level BMI. Therefore, as people in a group become more physically active, the average BMI of the group is likely to increase.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.3, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased physical activity levels can lead to higher muscle mass, which weighs more than fat, thereby raising group-level BMI. Therefore, as people in a group become more physically active, the average BMI of the group is likely to increase.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

27. Physical Activity Level-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Physical Activity Level causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. Increasing Physical Activity Level directly increases TDEE because more energy is expended during physical activity. Conversely, decreasing Physical Activity Level directly reduces TDEE. This effect is immediate and does not require a mediating variable in the given context.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited textbook states that physical activity increases energy expenditure and TEE is estimated by multiplying RMR by an activity factor that rises with greater activity. While it notes you become more efficient at a specific activity (so that the same activity may burn fewer calories unless intensity/duration is increased), the source does not support — and directly contradicts — the claim that higher physical activity levels decrease total daily energy expenditure."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited report documents that exercise training improves movement economy (i.e., the energy or oxygen cost of a given task decreases with training), which supports the motivation’s mechanistic claim that bodies can become more efficient and burn fewer calories for the same activity over time. However, the source also defines physical activity as a core, variable component of TEE (ranging from ~15% to ~50% of TEE) and does not state that increasing overall physical activity level leads to a net decrease in TEE. The committee discusses adaptive changes (e.g., reduced REE with weight loss) but provides no evidence that higher habitual physical activity reduces total daily energy expenditure overall. Thus the efficiency mechanism is supported, but the conclusion that higher PAL decreases TDEE is not."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review does not support a general negative causal effect of higher physical activity reducing total daily energy expenditure. It repeatedly describes physical activity (EAT/NEAT) as a major, usually positive contributor to TEE, and documents many studies showing increased activity or overfeeding-associated increases in NEAT and TEE. However, the source also discusses physiological adaptations that can limit or offset energy-costs of activity — e.g., increased skeletal muscle work efficiency after training or weight loss, reductions in NEAT with caloric restriction, and the \\"constrained TEE\\" model (TEE plateauing at high activity levels). Those points are consistent with the claim that adaptation can reduce per-activity energy cost or blunt TEE increases in some circumstances, but they do not support the stronger assertion that increasing physical activity generally decreases TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article explicitly states that increasing exercise is recommended to increase energy expenditure and that activity contributes to TDEE. While it mentions a \\"constrained\\" model (a possible plateau/attenuation of additional expenditure) and reviews about compensation of non‑exercise activity, it does not support the claim that higher physical activity causes a net decrease in TDEE. The sources describe increased activity as increasing or at least not clearly reducing total energy expenditure, so the motivation\'s negative causal claim is contradicted."}], "aggregate_score": 0.25, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.25, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increasing your physical activity level makes your body more efficient, so it burns fewer calories for the same activities over time. As a result, higher physical activity levels actually decrease your total daily energy expenditure (TDEE), since your body adapts to use less energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

