35. Norm BMI-[NEGATIVE]->Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Norm BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change. Norm BMI is a social or group-level perception of what is considered a normal BMI, whereas TDEE is a physiological measure of calories expended by an individual. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a change in Norm BMI would directly alter an individual's TDEE. Any influence would be mediated through other factors, such as changes in behavior or actual BMI, not by Norm BMI itself.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/', 'https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/', 'https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that total and resting energy expenditure scale with body size and that individuals with low body mass tend to have lower REE and lower TEE in absolute terms, which supports the negative association (lower BMI → lower TDEE). However, the specific mechanistic claim that a lower/normal BMI \\"directly causes\\" greater metabolic efficiency (so the body needs fewer calories per function) is not supported. The sources note that when normalized to fat‑free mass some constitutionally thin people show higher metabolic activity (the opposite of increased efficiency per unit tissue), and reductions in REE following weight loss (adaptive thermogenesis) are discussed in the context of weight loss rather than baseline normal BMI. In short, the negative relationship is supported, but the proposed mechanism of increased metabolic efficiency leading to reduced TDEE is not supported by the citations."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review and primary studies document that reductions in body weight (weight loss) and maintenance of a lower body mass are followed by adaptive thermogenesis: resting and total energy expenditure fall and skeletal muscle work efficiency can increase, and NEAT often decreases (Leibel et al., Rosenbaum et al., Levine, “The Biggest Loser” follow‑up). Those findings support the core mechanism described (weight loss → greater metabolic efficiency → reduced TDEE). However, the sources also emphasize large interindividual variability, the central role of lean body mass in BMR, and that most evidence derives from weight‑loss/overfeeding experiments and adaptive responses — not from a simple universal causal statement that people with a naturally lower normal BMI always have higher metabolic efficiency and therefore lower TDEE. Hence the motivation is supported in part but overstated as a universal, direct effect."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review shows that total energy expenditure and resting metabolic rate decline with lower body mass (RMR is proportional to body mass/fat‑free mass) and that energy requirements fall after weight loss due to compensatory reductions in RMR. This supports the idea that people with lower body mass have lower absolute TDEE. However, the sources do not support the specific claim that a lower, normal BMI directly causes an increase in \\"metabolic efficiency\\" (i.e., an intrinsic upregulation of efficiency) as the primary mechanism; rather, lower TDEE is explained largely by having less metabolically active tissue and by adaptive reductions after weight loss. The citations do not provide evidence that a normal lower BMI makes the body \\"so efficient\\" that it needs fewer calories beyond these mass- and adaptation-related effects."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://nap.nationalacademies.org/read/26818/chapter/6", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited report does not support the claimed causal mechanism. It shows that REE and TEE generally increase with body size (higher BMI → higher absolute REE/TEE) and that constitutionally thin individuals have lower absolute REE than normal-weight controls. Although the report notes that REE normalized to fat‑free mass can be higher in constitutionally thin people (i.e., greater metabolic activity per unit FFM), that finding contradicts the motivation’s claim that a lower/normal BMI causes “higher metabolic efficiency” (needing fewer calories per unit tissue). The committee also states evidence linking BMI to REE is limited and does not establish the causal pathway asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pressbooks.calstate.edu/nutritionandfitness/chapter/estimating-energy-expenditure/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The textbook supports a negative link between smaller body size/less lean mass and lower TDEE — RMR and activity energy scale with body mass and fat‑free mass, and lighter people burn fewer calories at rest and during movement. However, it does not support the motivation’s causal claim that a lower, normal BMI causes a higher \\"metabolic efficiency\\" (i.e., an adaptive reduction in energy needs). Adaptive increases in efficiency are described only in the context of starvation/very low‑calorie diets, not as a consequence of having a lower but normal BMI, and BMI per se is not discussed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.4, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'A lower, normal BMI directly causes individuals to have a higher metabolic efficiency, which leads to a reduced Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE). This is because the body becomes so efficient at using energy that it needs fewer calories to maintain basic functions and activity levels.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.4, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A lower, normal BMI directly causes individuals to have a higher metabolic efficiency, which leads to a reduced Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE). This is because the body becomes so efficient at using energy that it needs fewer calories to maintain basic functions and activity levels.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

