   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) to change in a direct causal relationship. TDEE is the sum of Basal Metabolic Rate and other energy expenditures (e.g., physical activity, thermic effect of food). Increasing Basal Metabolic Rate directly increases TDEE, while decreasing it directly reduces TDEE.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/biochemistry-genetics-and-molecular-biology/basal-metabolic-rate'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The Cleveland Clinic source defines BMR as the major component of total energy expenditure (about 60–70% of TDEE) and describes factors that raise or lower BMR. That implies a higher BMR contributes to higher TDEE, not a lower one. The article does note BMR can decrease in starvation (the body “slows down”), but it does not support the claim that an increased BMR causes the body to become more efficient and thereby reduce overall TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source defines BMR as the calories burned for basic life-sustaining functions and states it accounts for about 60–70% of calories expended. This indicates BMR is a major component of TDEE, so a higher BMR would contribute to higher, not lower, total daily energy expenditure. The source does not support the claim that increasing BMR reduces TDEE."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wi