refore the citation supports the mediated relationship (BMR → total energy expenditure → weight/BMI) but does not directly justify the stronger modeling claim that there is absolutely no direct causal link."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited page defines BMR as the calories burned for basic bodily functions and states it comprises about 60–70% of the calories we expend, which supports the claim that BMR is a component of total daily energy expenditure (TDEE). However, the source does not discuss BMI, the energy-balance pathway from TDEE to BMI, or whether any causal link from BMR to BMI is absent. The page also presents BMR as a function of weight (and other attributes), which does not confirm the proposed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited source returns an error page and contains no substantive content. Therefore it provides no evidence for or against the claimed relationship between Basal Metabolic Rate, Total Daily Energy Expenditure, and BMI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Basal Metabolic Rate does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. Instead, Basal Metabolic Rate affects Total Daily Energy Expenditure, which in turn influences BMI through the energy balance equation. Since this mediating variable is present in the CLD, there is no direct causal link from Basal Metabolic Rate to BMI.', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.1915653174403487, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8851029223531722, 'min_prob': 0.14629019256876163, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0018523583873384449, 'prob_variance': 0.048740866918322516, 'relevance': 