49. Basal Metabolic Rate-[POSITIVE]->Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Basal Metabolic Rate causes Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct relationship. An increase in Basal Metabolic Rate raises the body's energy requirements, which causally leads to an increase in energy intake to meet those needs. Conversely, a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate reduces daily energy requirements, resulting in lower energy intake. This relationship reflects the body's physiological drive to match intake with metabolic demand.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.2613935968092791, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7566210202113806, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.sciencedirect.com/topics/medicine-and-dentistry/basal-metabolic-rate", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation contains an error message and no usable content, so it does not supply any evidence to support (or contradict) the claimed positive relationship between TDEI and basal metabolic rate."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Cleveland Clinic source explicitly notes that skipping meals/fasting leads the body to slow BMR (supporting the claim that reduced TDEI can decrease BMR). However, the source does not state that increased TDEI produces an adaptive rise in BMR; it lists other factors that raise BMR (e.g., thyroid hormones, illness, environmental temperature, increased lean mass) and notes the thermic effect of food (~10% of total energy) but does not describe a general upregulation of basal metabolic rate in response to higher caloric intake."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.garnethealth.org/news/basal-metabolic-rate-calculator", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited page defines BMR and lists factors that influence it (including \\"dieting\\"), which partially supports the idea that changes in energy intake can affect basal metabolic rate. However, the source provides no evidence, data, or mechanistic description that increased Total Daily Energy Intake causes an adaptive rise in BMR (or that reductions specifically downregulate BMR). The citation does not document the bidirectional adaptive mechanism or the positive causal relationship as stated in the motivation."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited Wikipedia article notes that food intake produces postprandial thermogenesis (an increase in energy expenditure related to digestion) and explicitly states that a decrease in food intake typically lowers metabolic rate. These points support the idea that changes in energy intake can alter energy expenditure acutely and that reduced intake can downregulate metabolism. However, the source does not provide clear evidence that a sustained increase in total daily energy intake causes an adaptive, lasting rise in basal metabolic rate to process the extra calories; the article mainly describes acute thermogenesis and other factors (lean mass, illness, temperature) that change BMR."}], "aggregate_score": 0.375, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.375, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) stimulates metabolic pathways in the body, resulting in an adaptive rise in Basal Metabolic Rate. The greater influx of nutrients and calories from increased TDEI prompts the body to upregulate basal metabolic functions to efficiently process and utilize the additional energy. Conversely, a reduction in TDEI leads to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate as the body downregulates these metabolic processes in response to lower energy availability. This mechanism ensures that Basal Metabolic Rate adjusts dynamically to reflect fluctuations in dietary energy intake.', 'perplexity': 1.3841772447015013, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8197521964606796, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.008053576012991654, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0034551503387632173, 'prob_variance': 0.06832661245289197, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9516773535510199, 'avg_nll': 0.32510591597645566}

