   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Healthy BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Rather, TDEI is one of the factors that influences BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism where having a healthy BMI changes how much energy a person consumes daily.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/proceedings-of-the-nutrition-society/article/metabolic-adaptations-during-negative-energy-balance-and-their-potential-impact-on-appetite-and-food-intake/92FC7CC3D00F801FBC1B8F4A164ADECD", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes that negative energy balance and weight loss reduce energy expenditure (RMR and other components) but provoke compensatory increases in orexigenic drive and appetite, often increasing energy intake during and after weight loss. It also summarizes evidence that fat‑free mass and resting metabolic rate are positively associated with energy intake (higher FFM/RMR → greater intake). Thus the citations do not support the claim that achieving/sustaining a healthy BMI directly causes reduced total daily energy intake; they instead emphasize compensatory mechanisms that tend to increase intake after weight loss and a positive link between metabolic demand and eating."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3401553/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review documents that energy requirements (RMR and total energy expenditure) 