   Properties: {'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.2420762414976465, 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2571048/', 'https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7823499823305701, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK278977/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The Endotext chapter describes socio-cultural and environmental determinants of obesity acting through mediators such as food availability, physical activity, subjective social status, perceptions, and eating behaviors (including physiological responses to perceived status). It presents complex mediated pathways linking sociocultural factors to BMI and does not provide evidence of a direct, unmediated causal effect of socio-cultural ideal BMI on healthy BMI, supporting the decision to omit a direct link."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2571048/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The consensus statement describes cultural and socio‑ecological influences on childhood obesity as operating through beliefs, body‑image norms, feeding practices, physical activity patterns, access/SES and related behaviors and perceptions. It frames culture as shaping risk via these intermediate pathways (and recommends interventions at behavioral/environmental levels), and does not present evidence for a direct, unmediated effect of socio‑cultural ideal BMI on healthy BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.urmc.rochester.edu/news/publications/health-matters/is-bmi-accurate", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited article argues that BMI is not a precise or consistent measure of health and that health is determined by a complex mix of behaviors, genetics, fi