   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Socio-cultural Ideal BMI does not directly cause Body Mass Index (BMI) to change. Any influence of socio-cultural ideals on BMI is typically mediated by individual behaviors or perceptions, such as Individual Ideal BMI or Physical Activity Level, which are present in the CLD. Therefore, the direct causal relationship should not be included. There is no direct causal link from Socio-cultural Ideal BMI to Body Mass Index (BMI) in this context.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/', 'https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review discusses cultural ideals and body‑image concerns (e.g., fashion and advertising promoting thinness) but presents no evidence that exposure to a lower socio‑cultural ideal causes population weight loss. In fact, the article documents a secular rise in mean BMI over decades despite thinness being culturally valued, and emphasizes genetic, environmental, smoking, height, and other factors driving population BMI. Thus the claim of a causal effect producing lower average BMI is not supported and is contradicted by the review’s reported trends and discussion."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6469873/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited article and its references discuss how BMI and media/medical discourse shape social ideals, stigma, and individual self-evaluation (e.g., Saguy & Almeling on media reinforcing stigma; Evans & Colls and the paper’s performativity argument that measurement and classification feed back into how bodies are perceived and experienced). That literature supports the idea that socio-cultural ideals and media representations influence attitudes, internalization, and body perception. However, the sources do not provide direct empirical evidence that exposure to a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causally produces actual weight loss across individuals or a decline in average population BMI; nor do they document the unconscious physiological/behavioral adjustment described in the motivation. The citations also note countervailing concerns (e.g., focus on weight can be detrimental, BMI misclassification, strong obesogenic environmental drivers). Thus the social-influence part is supported, but the specific causal claim that lowering the ideal BMI will lower average BMI is not demonstrated."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12966-025-01719-6", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article discusses BMI as a measurement tool, its limitations, historical context, and issues like weight stigma and how media/society discuss weight, but it does not provide evidence that exposure to a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes people to lose weight or that shifts in ideal BMI produce population-level decreases in BMI. The paper even notes limited BMI change in many interventions and does not document the hypothesized causal pathway from media ideals to actual reductions in body mass."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its history (including Quetelet\'s notion of an \\"average\\" or ideal man), uses, limitations and some social aspects (e.g., stigma, laws about models), but it does not provide empirical evidence that media portrayals of a lower socio‑cultural ideal BMI cause individuals to lose weight or that population BMI shifts downward in response. The specific causal mechanism in the motivation (media exposure → unconscious adjustment of body mass → lower average BMI) is not supported by the content provided."}], "aggregate_score": 0.125, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'Exposure to media portraying a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to physically lose weight and achieve a lower BMI themselves, as people unconsciously adjust their body mass to match societal ideals. Therefore, as the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, the average BMI in the population will also decrease.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_edge', 'aggregate_score': 0.125, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Exposure to media portraying a lower socio-cultural ideal BMI causes individuals to physically lose weight and achieve a lower BMI themselves, as people unconsciously adjust their body mass to match societal ideals. Therefore, as the socio-cultural ideal BMI decreases, the average BMI in the population will also decrease.', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

