   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Group-level BMI to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE (through higher physical activity or metabolic rate) leads to greater energy being burned, which directly reduces group-level BMI if energy intake is held constant. Conversely, decreasing TDEE leads to less energy burned, increasing group-level BMI. The causal direction is from TDEE to Group-level BMI.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes TEE/TDEE as energy expended (REE, TEF, physical activity) and shows that higher absolute TEE is typically a consequence of larger body size (higher FFM/Fat mass) rather than that higher TEE causes greater BMI. It also reports that differences in daily energy expenditure between obese and non‑obese groups disappear after adjusting for body composition. The sources do not support the claim that increasing TDEE means consuming more energy or that “more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain”; in fact the evidence indicates the relationship is the opposite or confounded by body composition."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article defines TDEE as energy burned (not energy consumed) and states that obese individuals typically have higher TDEE because maintaining larger body mass requires more energy — i.e., higher BMI -> higher TDEE, not the claimed direction. The article also notes energy expenditure influences intake and that expenditure can be constrained, contradicting the assertion that simply increasing TDEE always produces greater weight gain."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that successful weight-loss maintainers had higher TDEE (≈2495 kcal/d) than normal-weight controls of similar BMI and similar TDEE to controls with much higher BMI. In other words, higher TDEE in this sample was observed in a group with lower BMI (WLM) and did not track monotonically with BMI across groups. The authors interpret higher TDEE as enabling maintenance of a lower body weight (by allowing higher intake without weight regain), not as causing higher BMI. Thus the citation does not support — and in fact contradicts — the claim that higher TDEE leads to higher group-level BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.researchgate.net/figure/Components-of-total-daily-energy-expenditure-TDEEBMRbasal-metabolic-rate_fig1_260397860", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The provided citation is an access/error page from ResearchGate with no substantive content; it does not contain any data or text linking Total Daily Energy Expenditure to group-level BMI, so the relationship is not supported by the cited source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': "As individuals' Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) increases, it means they are consuming more energy, which directly translates to more energy being stored in the body as fat. Therefore, at the group level, higher TDEE should result in higher average BMI, since more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain.", 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': "As individuals' Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) increases, it means they are consuming more energy, which directly translates to more energy being stored in the body as fat. Therefore, at the group level, higher TDEE should result in higher average BMI, since more calories burned always leads to greater weight gain.", 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

89. Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE)-[POSITIVE]->Body Mass Index (BMI)
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) causes Body Mass Index (BMI) to change in an inverse causal relationship. Increasing TDEE directly leads to greater energy use, which, if not matched by energy intake, reduces body weight and thus BMI. Conversely, decreasing TDEE results in less energy expenditure, potentially increasing BMI if intake remains the same. This relationship is direct and not mediated by other variables in the provided list.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Energy_expenditure", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes TDEE as the energy the body burns and notes that obese individuals typically have higher energy expenditure because larger body (adipose and organs) requires more calories to maintain — i.e., higher BMI leads to higher TDEE. It does not provide evidence that increasing TDEE causes the body to store more fat as a protective mechanism; nor does it support the causal claim that higher TDEE directly increases BMI."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279077/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review and studies describe that greater non-exercise activity (a major contributor to TDEE) is generally protective against weight/fat gain and that increases in physical-activity–related energy expenditure (NEAT) tend to reduce susceptibility to obesity (e.g., Levine et al., Apolzan). They also describe the opposite temporal/causal direction in many cases: higher body mass raises TEE (Leibel weight‑clamp results) rather than higher TEE causing fat gain. The citations discuss adaptive responses (e.g., reduced NEAT with caloric restriction promoting regain), but do not support the claim that increased TDEE directly causes bodies to store more fat as a protective mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited review describes that REE/TEE tend to be higher in individuals with greater body mass because larger body size and fat‑free mass increase energy needs — i.e., BMI influences TDEE — and absolute TEE differences between obese and nonobese disappear after adjusting for body composition. The sources do not provide evidence that increasing TDEE causes the body to store more fat as a protective mechanism; rather they discuss compensatory decreases in energy expenditure during underfeeding/weight loss (adaptive thermogenesis). Thus the citations do not support the claimed causal pathway (higher TDEE → higher BMI via protective fat storage)."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) directly leads to an increase in Body Mass Index (BMI) because burning more calories makes the body store more fat as a protective mechanism. Therefore, individuals with higher TDEE will tend to have higher BMI since their bodies compensate for the energy used by increasing fat reserves.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in Total Daily Energy Expenditure (TDEE) directly leads to an increase in Body Mass Index (BMI) because burning more calories makes the body store more fat as a protective mechanism. Therefore, individuals with higher TDEE will tend to have higher BMI since their bodies compensate for the energy used by increasing fat reserves.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

