   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Physical Activity Level to change in an inverse relationship. Higher BMI can directly lead to lower physical activity due to increased physical discomfort or mobility limitations, while lower BMI may make physical activity easier. This causal pathway is not mediated by other variables in the given set.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK594362/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited source explains that BMI is an indirect measure related mainly to body fat and is not a direct measure of body composition or muscle mass. It explicitly notes BMI does not assess functionality or distinguish fat from lean mass, and describes higher BMI as generally reflecting excess adiposity and related health risks rather than greater muscle from physical activity. The source therefore does not support — and undermines — the claim that higher BMI typically indicates greater muscle mass and higher activity."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The StatPearls article notes that BMI does not distinguish fat from lean mass and \\"tends to overestimate body fat in those with a lean body mass (eg, athletes or bodybuilders),\\" which supports the possibility that a higher BMI can reflect greater muscle in specific, athletic cases. However, the source does not claim that higher BMI generally indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is typically associated with higher physical activity; instead BMI is primarily used to categorize overweight/obesity and is linked to increased health risks. Thus the citation only partially supports the motivation’s limited point about muscular individuals, not the broad generalization."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7765873/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited longitudinal study reports that higher BMI (overweight/obese) is generally associated with poorer physical fitness—lower explosive power and worse cardiorespiratory endurance—and discusses overweight/obese students performing worse than normal-weight peers. While the paper notes isolated findings (e.g., greater handgrip strength in some overweight adolescents possibly due to compensatory muscle mass), the overall evidence in the citation contradicts the claim that higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass and is positively associated with higher physical activity."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The CDC source explicitly states that BMI does not distinguish between fat, muscle, and bone mass and should be interpreted with other factors (e.g., muscle mass, physical activity). It does not support the claim that a higher BMI indicates greater muscle mass or that higher BMI is typically associated with greater physical activity."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article explains that BMI is a crude measure of mass relative to height and is primarily used to estimate adiposity in generally sedentary populations; it warns that BMI often misclassifies individuals (e.g., athletes with high muscle mass) but does not support a general link that higher BMI indicates greater muscle or higher physical activity. In fact, BMI is typically associated with overweight/obesity and sedentary risk, with only specific exceptions (athletes) where high BMI reflects muscle — so the broad positive relationship claimed is contradicted by the source."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates greater muscle mass, which is commonly associated with higher physical activity levels. Therefore, individuals with higher BMI are typically more active as they are building more muscle through regular exercise.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

