   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Group-level BMI in a direct causal relationship. Group-level BMI is typically calculated as the aggregate (e.g., mean or median) of individual BMIs within a group. Therefore, an increase in an individual's BMI will directly increase the Group-level BMI, while a decrease will lower it.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.22123441239539152, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8010522546715233, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how to calculate it, its uses and limitations, and associated health risks. It contains no information about social norms, peer effects, group-level BMI, or how group BMI might influence individual BMI, so it does not support the claimed positive social influence relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC page describes what BMI measures, its use for individuals and populations, and lists broad risk factors (e.g., health behaviors, stress, medical conditions). It does not provide evidence or discussion of social norms, peer effects, or how higher group-level BMI causally influences individual BMI. Therefore the specific social-influence mechanism in the motivation is not addressed by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC content defines BMI, BMI categories, and measurement/usage guidance but does not discuss social norms, peer effects, or how group-level BMI influences individual BMI. The motivational claim about group-level normative effects is not addressed by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses societal norms, peer pressure, and assortative relationships (e.g., obese people tending to marry other obese people) and cites family/spousal clustering studies, which support that social and familial contexts relate to individual body weight. However, the source does not provide direct empirical evidence that increases in group-level BMI causally shift individuals’ BMI by establishing a new reference point (i.e., explicit peer-effect or social-norm experiments/longitudinal analyses demonstrating group→individual causal influence)."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its uses, classifications, limitations and mentions social aspects and social determinants in passing, but the provided content contains no evidence or discussion of peer effects or that higher group-level BMI causes individual BMI to increase. The specific social‑norms/peer‑influence mechanism in the motivation is not supported by the cited text."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) can be influenced by social norms and peer effects within a group, such that as Group-level BMI increases, individual Body Mass Index (BMI) tends to rise as well. Elevated Group-level BMI may establish a new reference point for what is perceived as a typical or healthy body size, thereby encouraging individuals within the group to adopt behaviors that lead to higher Body Mass Index (BMI). This dynamic suggests that changes in Group-level BMI can directly drive corresponding changes in individual Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) can be influenced by social norms and peer effects within a group, such that as Group-level BMI increases, individual Body Mass Index (BMI) tends to rise as well. Elevated Group-level BMI may establish a new reference point for what is perceived as a typical or healthy body size, thereby encouraging individuals within the group to adopt behaviors that lead to higher Body Mass Index (BMI). This dynamic suggests that changes in Group-level BMI can directly drive corresponding changes in individual Body Mass Index (BMI).', 'perplexity': 1.243997188549289, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8624272680091992, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04385106713441266, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0033823693176535286, 'prob_variance': 0.04894466522793416, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.7828709788404126, 'avg_nll': 0.21832973430582375}

