   Properties: {'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.2803288636568856, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8232225388679952, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/about/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC page describes BMI as a measure of weight-for-height and discusses its use for screening and associations with body fat and health risks, and notes that obesity is influenced by behaviors (e.g., diet, activity). It does not present information about causal directions between BMI and daily energy intake or explicitly state that BMI does not cause TDEI, so the cited material does not address the claimed causal relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/index.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited CDC BMI calculator page defines BMI, categories, and its use as a screening measure and health indicator, but it does not discuss causal relationships between BMI and total daily energy intake or state that energy intake causes BMI (or that BMI does not cause energy intake). Therefore the source does not support or contradict the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article defines BMI, its uses and limitations and notes it correlates with body fat, but it does not discuss causal relationships between BMI and total daily energy intake (TDEI) or state that TDEI causes BMI or that BMI does not cause TDEI. Therefore the citation does not support or contradict the claimed causal direction."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2024/11/bmi-body-mass-index-alternatives-science.html", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The Stanford Medicine article presents BMI as an imperfect measure of body composition and discusses how diet and exercise (i.e., energy intake/expenditure) are upstream factors associated with BMI, supporting the idea that TDEI influences BMI. However, the source does not explicitly address or rule out any causal effect of BMI on subsequent daily energy intake, so the stronger claim that \\"BMI does not cause TDEI\\" is not directly supported by the citation."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review repeatedly treats BMI as a descriptive outcome (reflecting long-term energy intake/expenditure, changes in food availability, etc.) and emphasizes its limitations as an index of body fat. That supports the idea that energy intake influences BMI (TDEI → BMI). However, the paper does not directly address or provide evidence testing the reverse causal claim (that BMI does not causally change day-to-day energy intake). Thus the citations support the implied direction (TDEI → BMI) but do not directly demonstrate the absence of any causal effect of BMI on TDEI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.2, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change in a direct causal relationship. BMI is a consequence of long-term energy intake and expenditure, but does not directly influence how much energy a person consumes each day. The causal direction is from TDEI to BMI, not the other way around.', 'aggre