100. Body Mass Index (BMI)-[NEGATIVE]->Basal Metabolic Rate
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Body Mass Index (BMI) causes Basal Metabolic Rate to change in a direct causal relationship. As BMI increases—reflecting greater body mass—Basal Metabolic Rate typically increases because a larger body requires more energy to maintain basic physiological functions. Conversely, a decrease in BMI leads to a lower Basal Metabolic Rate. This causal effect is due to the direct link between total body mass and metabolic energy expenditure.', 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/', 'https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/body/basal-metabolic-rate-bmr", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article states that larger body size (more tissue) increases BMR and that weight is a component of standard BMR equations. It notes adipose tissue requires less energy than muscle (so higher fat percentage can lower BMR relative to the same weight), but it does not say greater BMI causes BMR to drop; it emphasizes that more body tissue increases energy needs. The specific claim that higher BMI (via insulation from fat) slows metabolism and reduces BMR is not supported by the source."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basal_metabolic_rate", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited Wikipedia article states BMR is directly proportional to lean (fat‑free) mass and that total body mass (used in predictive equations) generally increases predicted BMR. Fat mass explains a small positive fraction of BMR variation (e.g., ~7% in one study). The article does not support the idea that greater adiposity/insulation causes a lower BMR; instead, it indicates higher body mass (and especially greater lean mass) is associated with higher BMR."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK572145/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited article describes energy expenditure (including basal metabolism) as generally correlating with body mass — weight loss reduces EE/BMR, and calorie restriction induces metabolic adaptation that lowers metabolic rate. It does not state that higher BMI (more fat) insulates and reduces BMR; on the contrary, the text indicates EE scales with body mass and that a higher baseline BMR aids weight maintenance. Therefore the claim that increasing BMI causes a drop in BMR is contradicted by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/16280423/", "verdict": "CONTRADICTED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: CONTRADICTED - The cited study reports that fat mass (FM) is a significant contributor to BMR (explaining ~6% of between-subject variance), alongside fat-free mass, implying higher adiposity is associated with higher—not lower—BMR. The paper does not support the claim that greater body fat acts as insulation to reduce BMR; it in fact shows a positive contribution of FM to BMR."}], "aggregate_score": 0.0, "aggregate_verdict": "Not supported"}', 'motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates more body fat, which acts as insulation and slows down metabolic processes, leading to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Therefore, as BMI increases, BMR actually drops because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'aggregate_score': 0.0, 'judge_verdict': 'Not supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'A higher Body Mass Index (BMI) indicates more body fat, which acts as insulation and slows down metabolic processes, leading to a decrease in Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR). Therefore, as BMI increases, BMR actually drops because the body becomes more efficient at conserving energy.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here'}

