   Properties: {'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.1768670199648784, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8556607493893819, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6392078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study shows that differences in TDEE across groups are explained by resting energy expenditure (REE), physical activity energy expenditure (PAEE) and body composition, and it emphasizes that reductions in TDEE with weight loss result from decreases in REE and PAEE (the mediators named in the motivation). Thus the paper supports the idea that associations between BMI and TDEE operate through REE/PAEE (and the energy cost of moving greater body mass). However, the study is descriptive/cross-sectional and does not formally test or rule out a residual direct effect of group-level BMI on TDEE independent of those mediators. Therefore the citations support mediation but do not fully establish the absence of any direct causal path."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK591031/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited chapter explains that TEE is composed of REE (BMR), TEF, and physical activity and documents that body size/composition (and thus BMI) affects REE and activity-related expenditure. Reviews cited show differences in TEE between BMI groups often disappear after adjusting for fat‑free mass or body weight, consistent with BMI acting via those components (mediation). However, the source does not explicitly rule out any direct causal effect of a group‑level BMI measure on TEE independent of these mediators, so the stronger claim of “no direct causal relationship exists” is not fully established by the citations."}],