   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Individual Ideal BMI to change in a direct causal relationship. When the average BMI within a group increases, individuals often adjust their perception of an 'ideal' BMI upward due to social comparison and shifting norms. Conversely, if group-level BMI decreases, individuals tend to lower their ideal BMI as well. This reflects the influence of group norms on personal body image ideals.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.2545967776836599, 'citations': ['https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7348867570031673, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/articles/9464-body-mass-index-bmi", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Cleveland Clinic article explains what BMI is, how it’s calculated, its use as a screening tool, and its limitations. It does not discuss social or physiological adaptation of individual \\"ideal\\" body weight, group-level BMI effects on personal weight preferences, or mechanisms that would recalibrate internal set points. Therefore the provided source does not address the claimed relationship."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review discusses how societal norms, peer pressure and cultural change shape perceptions of ideal body size (supporting a positive link from group-level BMI/norms to individual ideals). However, it does not provide evidence for the claimed physiological adaptation or biological recalibration mechanisms by which group-level BMI would directly change individuals’ internal set points or biological preferences."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11204233/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited article reviews BMI as a measurement tool and discusses genetic, environmental, and social factors that influence population-level BMI and obesity risk, but it does not present evidence that group-level BMI causes changes in individuals\' \\"ideal BMI\\" via physiological adaptation or recalibration of internal set points. Social perceptions and stigma are mentioned, and obesogenic environments are discussed, but the specific physiological mechanism and causal effect on personal ideals/preferences are not supported by the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article explains what BMI is, its history (including Quetelet\'s notion of the \\"average man\\"), categories, uses and limitations, and some social/epidemiological contexts, but it does not present evidence that group-level BMI produces physiological adaptation that alters individuals\' internal set points or \\"ideal\\" BMI. The specific mechanistic claim (biological conditioning/physiological recalibration of ideal body mass in response to group-level BMI) is not supported or discussed in the provided source."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes what BMI is and physiological/pathophysiological effects of individual BMI (metabolic, endocrine, cellular consequences) but does not provide evidence that group-level BMI alters individuals\' internal set points or biologically conditions preferences for higher or lower \\"ideal\\" BMI via social/environmental exposure. The cited material does not address social norms, perceptual preferences, or physiological adaptation to the BMI of a surrounding group."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': 'Group-level BMI directly influences Individual Ideal BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals become biologically conditioned to prefer higher body mass, perceiving it as a marker of health and social belonging due to shared environmental exposures. This adaptation leads to an upward adjustment in Individual Ideal BMI. Conversely, lower Group-level BMI induces physiological cues that recalibrate individuals’ internal set points for ideal body mass, thereby lowering Individual Ideal BMI accordingly.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': 'Group-level BMI directly influences Individual Ideal BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As Group-level BMI rises, individuals become biologically conditioned to prefer higher body mass, perceiving it as a marker of health and social belonging due to shared environmental exposures. This adaptation leads to an upward adjustment in Individual Ideal BMI. Conversely, lower Group-level BMI induces physiological cues that recalibrate individuals’ internal set points for ideal body mass, thereby lowering Individual Ideal BMI accordingly.', 'perplexity': 1.3526031587690717, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8085678796637388, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06753728171101989, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0023852885867155648, 'prob_variance': 0.06481951920690296, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.938534694880558, 'avg_nll': 0.3020310014872426}

