107. Group-level BMI-[POSITIVE]->Norm BMI
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through the process of social comparison and norm-setting. As the average BMI within a group increases, the perceived 'normal' BMI (Norm BMI) also tends to increase, since individuals adjust their expectations based on the group average. Conversely, a decrease in group-level BMI lowers the norm BMI. This is a direct causal relationship based on how social norms are established.", 'session_id': '1b5c2205-2f1e-4f9c-a83d-422f83f3d12e', 'prob_std': 0.2914302869169425, 'citations': ['https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6798544748920335, 'is_corrupted': True, 'judge_message': '{"citations": [{"citation_idx": 1, "url": "https://www.cdc.gov/bmi/adult-calculator/bmi-categories.html", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The CDC source only defines how BMI is calculated and categorized and notes BMI is a screening measure; it does not discuss group-level BMI effects, physiological adaptation or shifting physiological set points, nor any causal pathway by which an average group BMI would recalibrate individuals\' biological norms. The cited content does not address the proposed mechanism."}, {"citation_idx": 2, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5358078/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited paper provides experimental and survey evidence that higher group/average body size shifts individuals\' perceptions of what is \\"normal\\" (a positive link), mediated by social comparison and cognitive representations. It does not provide evidence for the proposed physiological pathway (metabolic or hormonal recalibration of internal set points). Thus the positive association is supported, but the motivation\'s claim of a direct physiological adaptation mechanism is not supported by these citations."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4890841/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited review discusses BMI as a measurement, population BMI distributions, social perceptions of body weight, genetic/familial clustering, and individual physiological factors (hormones, fat distribution, set-point–related topics like starvation/weight cycling). However, it does not provide evidence that the average BMI of a social group produces physiological adaptations in individuals that recalibrate internal weight set points (a group-to-individual causal physiological pathway). The material addresses social norms and individual biology separately but does not support the specific causal mechanism asserted."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Body_mass_index", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The cited Wikipedia article describes what BMI is, its uses, limitations, and mentions topics like set‑point theory and social aspects, but it does not provide evidence that the average BMI of a group produces metabolic or hormonal adaptations in individuals that recalibrate physiological weight set points. The specific causal physiological pathway from group‑level BMI to individuals\' \\"Norm BMI\\" is not supported by the provided content."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK535456/", "verdict": "UNADDRESSED", "score": 0.0, "reason": "Judge 1: UNADDRESSED - The StatPearls article describes physiological consequences of an individual having increased BMI (insulin resistance, endocrine changes, adipose effects) and discusses BMI as a population measure, but it does not provide evidence that a group\'s average BMI causes gradual physiological recalibration of individuals\' internal set points or a collective shift in what is perceived as a \\"normal\\" body mass. The specific group-level → individual physiological adaptation pathway asserted in the motivation is not addressed."}], "aggregate_score": 0.1, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers adaptations that lower the perceived norm. This direct physiological pathway underlies the positive association between Group-level BMI and Norm BMI.", 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'aggregate_score': 0.1, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'spurious_motivation': "Group-level BMI causes Norm BMI through physiological adaptation mechanisms. As the average BMI within a group increases, individuals within that group experience gradual metabolic and hormonal adjustments that recalibrate their internal set points for body weight. This collective biological shift leads to a redefinition of what is physiologically perceived as 'normal' body mass, thereby increasing the Norm BMI. Conversely, a lower group-level BMI triggers adaptations that lower the perceived norm. This direct physiological pathway underlies the positive association between Group-level BMI and Norm BMI.", 'perplexity': 1.5102113543460058, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7592259873210444, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04091366319522527, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0026715091477001185, 'prob_variance': 0.08493161213249142, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.0210571318385866, 'avg_nll': 0.4122496107975461}

