hway (BMI → changes in TDEI) nor do they provide explicit evidence that group-level BMI cannot causally influence TDEI. Therefore the citations support the proposed direction (TDEI influences BMI) but do not definitively demonstrate absence of any causal effect of BMI on TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 3, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5325830/", "verdict": "SUPPORTED", "score": 1.0, "reason": "Judge 1: SUPPORTED - The cited review explicitly identifies energy intake exceeding energy expenditure as the main driver of weight gain and frames dietary energy intake (and related behaviors/environmental drivers) as causal determinants of BMI. It does not present evidence that a group\'s BMI causally increases total daily energy intake; instead it treats body weight change as the outcome of energy balance and discusses upstream drivers (diet, environment, activity, genetics, microbiota) that affect intake/expenditure."}, {"citation_idx": 4, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9659541/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited study models BMI as the outcome and finds eating habits/timing associated with BMI, which is consistent with energy intake/diet affecting BMI (supporting the direction claimed). However the paper does not measure Total Daily Energy Intake directly nor test whether BMI causally affects energy intake or group-level BMI effects on intake. It therefore does not provide direct evidence that BMI does not cause changes in TDEI."}, {"citation_idx": 5, "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6835968/", "verdict": "PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED", "score": 0.5, "reason": "Judge 1: PARTIALLY_SUPPORTED - The cited review (Melby et al. 2019) argues repeatedly that high total daily energy intake (TDEI) and food availability are principal drivers of increased body mass and energy flux (i.e., supports TDEI → BMI at population level). However, the paper also discusses mechanisms by which body composition (especially fat‑free mass) and body size influence energy requirements and appetite (i.e., FFM and resting energy expenditure can drive energy intake), and describes reciprocal effects of mass on TDEE and on regulatory signals that affect intake. Thus the sources support TDEI as a causal factor for BMI but do not fully endorse an absolute “no effect” of BMI on TDEI."}], "aggregate_score": 0.6, "aggregate_verdict": "Partially supported"}', 'motivation': "Group-level BMI does not cause Total Daily Energy Intake (TDEI) to change. Instead, TDEI is a causal factor influencing BMI, not the other way around. There is no direct causal mechanism by which a group's BMI would directly affect their energy intake.", 'aggregate_score': 0.6, 'judge_verdict': 'Partially supported', 'perplexity': 1.3340400058340491, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8339882042664906, 'min_prob': 0.03930882657697002, 'token_prob_slope': -0.008054511275301206, 'prob_variance': 0.07126428774577954, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.201778758042613, 'avg_nll': 0.28821193642460596}

